AB  - Introduction Fascia consists of several layers of a connective tissue network and is the main support for the body, continuance of the tissue sliding, and also serves as a way for nerves and vessels. Surgical trauma usually causes a break in this tissue, which subsequently leads inflammation and fibrosis over the damaged area, which also reduces the sliding and causes pain after the operation. Even though it plays a major biomechanical role, the remodeling of the fascia is barely visible in the perioperative anatomy and rehab. Material and method This narrative review involved search in PubMed, Scopus, Web of Science, and Google Scholar databases for articles published between 2000 and 2025. The search terms that were used included “fascia,” “fascial remodeling,” “post-surgical fibrosis,” “thoracolumbar fascia,” “uterine niche,” and “fascial-plane block.” Studies that reported postoperative structural or functional changes of the fascia were considered for inclusion. Results The response of fibroinflammatory nature mediated by TGF-Β after the surgical trauma of the tissue is characterized by the activation of myofibroblasts, the deposition of collagen, the reduction of glide as well as stiffness. On their turn, these changes lead to arthrofibrosis, thickening of the thoracolumbar fascia, nociception caused by traction, and cesarean scar niches. Very often, imaging shows that the fascia is swollen or has some defects. Pain relief through fascial-plane blocks and early rehabilitation are the methods that help in the restoration of the glide as well as in pain relief. Conclusion Fascial remodeling after surgery is the cause of many clinical issues and as a result surgery that conserves fascia, application of analgesics that are directed to the area, and early rehabilitation are necessary.
