AB  - Aim: Istmocele is associated with focal insufficiency of the scar, most often due to damaged microvasculature, most likely caused by cesarean section (CS) technique and local inflammatory response caused by hysterorrhaphy and peritonization. The aim of the paper is to show the incidence of istmocele in Habek's and Dörfler's technique CS technique. Material and methods: Habek's CS technique is a modification of Stark's (Misgav Ladach) CS method, which includes: Joel Cohen's or Pfannenstiel's laparotomy, isthmic transverse supraplical incision without bladder dissection, hysterotomy suturing with extraendometrial single-row non-locking suture without visceral and parietal peritonization. Dörfler's technique in specific cases included locking hysterorrhaphy with visceral and parietal peritonization. A retrospective clinical study using ultrasound diagnostics verified 24 postoperative isthmoceles during II. trimesters of the current pregnancy, which were clinically unmanifest in pregestation and were found anecdotally during examinations during pregnancy. Results: All isthmoceles were after one CS according to the Dörfler technique with locking hysterotomy suture, of which 18 cases were elective and 6 were emergency CS. In all cases, iterative CS was performed before the onset of labor (prelabour CS) from 36-39 weeks of gestation (mean 37.1). In 21 cases (87.5 %) an incomplete uterine rupture was found, while in 3 cases (12.5 %) a complete uterine rupture with amniocele was found. Conclusion: Non-locking single-row extension hysterorrhaphy without bladder dissection and peritonization according to the Habek CS technique directly correlates with scar sufficiency and the absence of isthmocele.
