AB  - Growing interest exists in immunological parallels between pregnancy and tumorigenesis, particularly regarding immune tolerance. However, ?direct comparative analyses of immune microenvironments in these contexts remain limited, leaving unresolved whether shared or distinct mechanisms drive T cell dysfunction. Endometrioid endometrial carcinoma (EEC), sharing ontogenic origin with pregnancy-associated endometrium (decidua), provides a unique model to dissect divergent immune adaptation strategies. Materials & Methods We integrated single-cell RNA and single-cell TCR sequencing (n=73,378 CD45+ cells), TCGA-UCEC datasets, and spatial transcriptomics, and compared CD8? T cell states emerge from normal endothelium, early decidua and EEC. Our analysis focused on T cell lineage, developmental trajectory, and cell-cell communication. Spatial mapping and antigen-matched pregnancy and tumor murine models were used to compare fetal/tumor antigen-specific CD8? T cell dynamics and phenotypic and functional features. Results This study presents the first multi-omics comparison of tumor-associated CD8? T cells (tCD8?) and decidual CD8? T cells (dCD8?). Tumor-infiltrating CD8? T cells (tCD8?) exhibited terminal exhaustion (PD-1/TOX/CD39hi) with clonal expansion from chronic TCR activation, while decidual CD8? T cells (dCD8?) maintained quiescent anergy with smaller clonotype via sparse TCR signaling. Mechanistically, EEC's immunosuppressive niche was shaped by clustered SPP1? macrophages and cDC2s engaging exhausted CD8? T cells via PD-L1-PD-1, Tim3-Glactin-9, Siglec-10-CD24, and CTLA4-CD80/86 axes, whereas scattered decidual FOLR2? macrophages and cDC1s restrained T cell activation through attenuated CD28-CD80/86 and CD40-CD40LG co-stimulation. Crucially, antigen priming routes also diverge between tumor and pregnancy̶circulating fetal antigens are captured by less matured CD11chigh MHC-IIlow/int resident DCs in distal lymph nodes to restrain maternal responses, while tumor antigens were presented by more matured migratory CD11clow/int MHC-IIhigh DCs via LYVE1? lymphatics. Functional validation demonstrated that draining lymph node-resident tumor-specific CD8? T cell reservoirs, instead of tumor-infiltrating T cell, better predicted survival, while adoptive transfer or conditional depletion of DCs bidirectionally modulated tumor control and fetal tolerance by reprogramming antigen-specific CD8? T cell functionality. Conclusion This study delineates divergent T cell tolerance mechanisms underlying maternal-fetal coexistence and tumor progression. By uncovering organ-specific immunological adaptations in uterine microenvironments, we establish DC-targeted therapeutic paradigms to dismantle tumor-exhaustion circuits without perturbing fetal tolerance. These findings provide crucial insights for developing fertility-sparing immunotherapies in gynecologic oncology.
