AB  - In the last two decades there has been increasing awareness and diagnosis of uterine caesarean scar niche or defect (CSN/ CSD). Most CSNs are asymptomatic but many medium-and long-term symptoms/complications are well recognised including caesarean scar ectopic pregnancy, placenta praevia accreta and scar dehiscence in subsequent pregnancies. However, very early postpartum symptomatic presentation of CSN has is mostly unknown and may go unrecognised. This review article describes cases of mild / severe secondary postpartum haemorrhage and persistent lochia-like discharge resulting from CSN encountered in a hospital in UK. A successful surgical repair of a CSN eight months postpartum with resolution of symptoms is described. The review deliberates appropriate diagnostic and management guidelines for the rare cases described. Failure of diagnosis can lead to avoidable hysterectomy as seen in a couple of cases. Hence, future reviews and guidelines on CSN should include the rare early postpartum presentations to improve awareness and appropriate management of these cases. Equally importantly, the findings in these cases strongly support the ‘ischemia of myometrial edges’ as the main and foremost mechanism of niche formation, which should henceforth be included in aetiological description of CSN. Aetiological hypotheses of poor myometrial apposition or inclusion endometrium in the suturing could not explain these cases and hence seem of next importance. Varying degrees of ‘ischemic necrosis’ seems an unintended consequence of misjudged/defensive excessively tight uterine incision suturing. Addressing this factor seems the best hope of reducing the incidence of CSN in future. Clinical studies in this regard are highly desirable but, in the meanwhile, obstetricians should reflect critically on the surgical technique and consider minimising myometrial devascularisation by avoiding extra-wide, interlocking and excessively tight suturing of myometrial edges.
DO  - 10.33545/gynae.2026.v10.i3k.2353
