AB  - Objective: With the increasing number of cesarean sections in recent years, a postoperative depression of the uterine scar(isthmocele)has drawn growing clinical attention. This defect, now recognized as Cesarean Scar Disorder(CSDi), can lead to chronic abnormal uterine bleeding, dysmenorrhea, pelvic pain, and even secondary infertility. In this report, we report on the indications, surgical techniques, and postoperative outcomes of patients diagnosed with CSDi who underwent cesarean scar defect repair at our institution. Methods: We analyzed 10 patients with cesarean scar defects who visited our institution between January 2022 and April 2025. Cesarean scar defects were identified by transvaginal ultrasonography and/or magnetic resonance imaging. Surgical indications for cesarean scar defect repair included cases of CSDi presenting with unexplained secondary infertility accompanied by persistent abnormal uterine bleeding or abnormal vaginal discharge, together with intrauterine fluid accumulation. The surgical procedure was laparoscopic repair of cesarean scar defects, performed with the aid of a flexible hysteroscope and intra-abdominal ultrasonography under laparoscopy. Postoperative outcomes were evaluated in terms of symptom improvement, complications, and effects on fertility. Results: Ten patients with cesarean scar defects and unexplained secondary infertility were diagnosed with CSDi and underwent laparoscopic repair. In most of the operated cases, postoperative improvement was observed in abnormal uterine bleeding and abnormal vaginal discharge. In addition, intrauterine fluid retention was reduced or resolved. Conclusion: Laparoscopic repair of cesarean scar defects for CSDi not only improved symptoms but also appeared to be an effective treatment option for patients desiring future fertility.
