AB  - Introduction For rapid and secure hemostasis of the lower uterine segment following placental evacuation in placenta previa, we have developed two novel techniques. The Hot-Dog method combines vertical compression sutures of the lower uterine segment with intrauterine balloon tamponade, while the Hot-Sand technique involves upward placement of the intrauterine balloon during uterine incision closure to prevent balloon damage. We report a case of placenta previa in which substantial hemorrhage was successfully controlled using the Hot-Dog method in conjunction with continuous barbed suturing for uterine closure with the Hot-Sand technique. Case A 27-year-old primigravida with placenta previa underwent elective cesarean section at 36 weeks of gestation. The placenta was located on the posterior uterine wall, completely covering the internal os. Massive bleeding occurred following placental evacuation. Hemostasis was achieved with the Hot-Dog method, followed by uterine closure using a barbed suture(STRATAFIX™ Spiral)with the HotSand technique. Total blood loss was 775 g, and operative time was 38 minutes. No balloon damage was observed during the procedure. Postoperatively, there was no significant vaginal bleeding, and the balloon was removed on day 1. Her postoperative course was uneventful. Conclusions Barbed sutures offer advantages over conventional continuous and interrupted suturing techniques by shortening operative time and lowering the risk of niche formation and cesarean scar defects. In placenta previa, combining barbed suturing with the Hot-Sand technique and the Hot-Dog method may minimize balloon-related complications and help achieve complete hemostasis. Written informed consent for this presentation was obtained from the patient.
