AB  - OBJECTIVE: Prior studies on isthmocele have largely relied on retrospective data, often with limited characterization and inconsistent methods of assessment. In this study, we aim to prospectively estimate the baseline prevalence of isthmoceles and describe their characteristics and associated endometrial findings in patients undergoing fertility evaluation. MATERIALS AND METHODS: Prospective, systematic assessment was performed using saline infusion sonogram (SIS) to evaluate for the presence or absence of isthmocele in patients with prior cesarean delivery (CD) at a single academic center. Isthmocele was defined as any defect observed at the level of the hysterotomy scar, with dimensions measured uniformly at time of SIS. Uterine flexion and concomitant lesions were documented. Baseline characteristics, infertility diagnoses, and CD-related variables were collected. Univariable logistic regression was used to examine associations between isthmocele development and factors including age, body mass index (BMI), infertility diagnosis, number of prior CDs, CD indication, estimated blood loss (EBL), chorioamnionitis, presence of fibroids or adenomyosis, and uterine flexion. Distributions of endometrial thickness measurements were compared using Kruskal-Wallis test. Analyses were conducted using Stata v18.5. RESULTS: A total of 100 patients with prior CD underwent SIS. The median age was 39 (interquartile range; IQR 37-41) and the median BMI was 25.2 (IQR 22.1-29.3). Most patients (92%) had 1 prior CD, while 5% had 2 prior CDs and 3% had 3 prior CDs. The prevalence of isthmocele was 37% (37/100). Of those with an isthmocele, 89.2% (33/37) had one prior CD and 10.8% (4/37) had 2+ prior CDs. The median isthmocele depth was 2.8mm (IQR 2.0-3.9). Median isthmocele width was 7.3mm (IQR 4.4-8.6). Median residual myometrial thickness was 7.7mm (IQR 5.6-9.3). Age, BMI, infertility diagnosis, number of CDs, CD indication, EBL, chorioamnionitis and the presence of fibroids/adenomyosis were not significantly associated with presence of isthmocele (all p > 0.05). Compared to anteflexion, retroflexion of the uterus was associated with decreased odds of isthmocele (OR 0.18; 95% CI 0.04-0.82; p = 0.03). To date, 32 patients had endometrial lining assessment following SIS in preparation for embryo transfer: 22 without isthmocele, and 10 with isthmocele. Distributions of endometrial lining thickness did not differ by isthmocele status (p = 0.67). Only one patient, who did not have an isthmocele, was identified to have intracavitary fluid. CONCLUSIONS: Isthmocele is a relatively common finding in patients with infertility with prior CD. Uterine retroflexion may be associated with decreased odds of isthmocele. In this prospective cohort to date, isthmocele did not significantly affect endometrial thickness, and intracavitary fluid was uncommon. IMPACT STATEMENT: Our prospective study could provide new insights into the prevalence, characteristics, and predictors of isthmocele following CD. Ongoing enrollment and data collection within this cohort are underway to confirm these findings and to further define their clinical implications in fertility treatment. SUPPORT: None
DO  - 10.1016/j.fertnstert.2025.07.102
