AB  - Study Objective: To demonstrate a comprehensive approach to laparoscopic repair of cesarean scar defects, incorporating round ligament suspension, while also addressing key patient counselling considerations related to fertility and future pregnancy. Design: Step-by-step explanation of the surgical technique accompanied by annotated surgical video footage. Setting: Academic tertiary care medical center. Patients or Participants: 40-year-old patient with a symptomatic cesarean scar defect and a residual myometrial thickness of 1 mm, desiring future pregnancy. Interventions: Operative laparoscopy performed using a systematic, stepwise technique, including bladder dissection, identification and delineation of the niche, excision of scar tissue, layered closure of the myometrium, and round ligament suspension to optimize uterine positioning. Measurements and Primary Results: Postoperative imaging demonstrated a significant reduction in niche size and an increase in residual myometrial thickness, enhancing the uterine wall for future pregnancy. Conclusion: Laparoscopic repair is the preferred surgical approach for symptomatic cesarean scar defects with residual myometrial thickness less than 3 mm in women desiring future pregnancy.
DO  - 10.1016/j.jmig.2025.09.407
