AB  - Study Objective: The goal of this study is to highlight the importance of including isthmocele as a potential diagnosis when presented with a pelvic mass. Design: This is a case report describing an isthmocele, presenting as a pelvic mass. This report describes the chief complaints, evaluation, and surgical management of a large isthmocele. The patient was seen for postoperative follow up, and is scheduled for a repeat pelvic MRI 3 months following surgery. Setting: The case was performed in the operating room. The patient was placed in the dorsal lithotomy position with arms tucked at the side and legs placed in stirrups. She was prepped and draped in the usual fashion. The patient was in Trendelenburg position throughout the case. Patients or Participants: The decision to create a surgical video occurred after the case was completed with the diagnosis of a large isthmocele and abnormal tissue at the site of interest. The patient was approached and consented several days after completion of the case, and after obtaining consent, a written form was completed and placed in the patient chart. Interventions: Robotic assisted laparoscopic surgery and hysteroscopy were used to diagnose and excise the isthmocele. Measurements and Primary Results: Multiple imaging modalities, including ultrasound and MRI, were used to diagnose the pelvic mass, likely causing infertility. On imaging, the isthmocele had features consistent with endometrioma and leiomyoma. Surgery was the definitive step which allowed the diagnosis of an isthmocele. Conclusion: It is imperative to consider isthmocele as a diagnosis when presented with a pelvic mass of uncertain etiology.
DO  - 10.1016/j.jmig.2025.09.098
