AB  - Study Objective: To determine trainee and fellowship director experience, perceptions, and preferences with simulation, and to assess the status of simulation training in FMIGS programs. Design: Survey-based study Setting: REDCap survey Patients or Participants: Current AAGL MIGS Fellows and Program Directors/Assistant Program Directors were surveyed. Interventions: A voluntary and anonymous survey was distributed before and after the Bootcamp session in 2023. Measurements and Primary Results: 163 respondents completed the pre-survey, 81 completed the post-survey. While only 5.52% of respondents reported formal simulation training as part of their fellowship curriculum, 90.60% noted access to a simulation center at their institution, and 93.87% reported participating in simulation either as learners or educators. The frequency of simulation training varied, with most (39.88%) participating 3-4 times yearly. The most commonly addressed clinical competency is psychomotor and surgical skills. Commonly cited barriers to implementing a formal simulation curriculum included lack of time (37.4%), faculty availability (26.4%), and training in simulation (19%). Despite these challenges, 67.5% of respondents indicated a formal FMIGS simulation curriculum would be important, very important, or imperative to their training, and 82.2% expressed willingness to participate in a national simulation curriculum. In the post-Bootcamp survey, clinical competencies that respondents identified should be more frequently addressed in fellowship training were team training (87.7%), critical thinking and decision-making skills (75.3%), and interpersonal communication skills (72.8%). The skills most frequently identified for inclusion in future simulation training included hysteroscopic septum or isthmocele resection (72.8%), laparoscopic retroperitoneal dissection (67.9%), and laparoscopic myomectomy (64.2%). The most valued aspects of simulation training for future inclusion were surgical education curriculum design and development (76.5%), simulation scenario design (72.8%), assessment and feedback (64.2%), and debriefing (63%). Conclusion: The information learned from this study should guide continued improvement in the FMIGS curriculum, Fellows Bootcamp, and overall simulation experience of FMIGS Fellows.
DO  - 10.1016/j.jmig.2025.09.062
