AB  - Study Objective: To demonstrate the key surgical steps of robotic-assisted uterine niche repair and highlight its effectiveness in symptom resolution and anatomical restoration. Design: Video case report of a robotic niche repair procedure with integrated hysteroscopic guidance. Setting: A university teaching hospital. Patients or Participants: A 27-year-old woman, four months postpartum after an uncomplicated cesarean section for prolonged second-stage labor, presenting with persistent foul-smelling vaginal discharge. Imaging revealed a fluid-filled cesarean scar with a necrotic area and 1.4 mm residual myometrial thickness, consistent with niche formation. Interventions: Robotic-assisted niche repair was performed five months post-cesarean. The procedure included bladder mobilization using a Hohl manipulator, peritoneal incision, bladder dissection, and external confirmation of the defect using hysteroscopic illumination. Debridement with vasopressin injection was followed by multilayer closure of the defect using absorbable sutures to restore myometrial and serosal continuity. Measurements and Primary Results: Postoperative recovery was uneventful. At six-week follow-up, the patient reported normalized menstruation and symptom resolution. Sonohysterography confirmed complete healing of the niche. Conclusion: Robotic-assisted repair of uterine niche is a safe and effective surgical approach that offers enhanced visualization and precision, resulting in excellent anatomical and symptomatic outcomes with minimal complications.
DO  - 10.1016/j.jmig.2025.09.418
