AB  - Study Objective: To assess the utility of intraoperative indocyanine green (ICG) fluorescence imaging in the surgical management of endometriotic lesions involving isthmocele, focusing on the identification and excision of the istimocel region. Design: Retrospective case report. Setting: High-volume tertiary referral center with expertise in minimally invasive gynecologic surgery and endometriosis. Patients or Participants: Premenopausal women diagnosed with symptomatic isthmocele-associated endometriosis, presenting with pelvic pain, dysmenorrhea and infertility. Interventions: Laparoscopic excision of endometriotic lesions located in and around the isthmocele, with the aid of intravesical ICG for enhanced intraoperative visualization of scar region and precise dissection. Measurements and Primary Results: Primary outcomes included surgical completeness (as confirmed by fluorescence-guided dissection), postoperative symptom relief (VAS scores for pain), and complication rates. ICG allowed for clear delineation of affected tissues and improved contrast with surrounding myometrium and serosa. All patients experienced marked improvement in symptoms, and no intraoperative or postoperative complications were observed. Conclusion: The use of ICG fluorescence imaging during laparoscopic treatment of isthmocele-associated endometriosis enhances intraoperative visualization, facilitates complete excision of endometriotic lesions, and may improve patient outcomes with minimal morbidity.
DO  - 10.1016/j.jmig.2025.09.427
