AB  - Introduction: In this work we present a case of caesarean scar pregnancy (CSP) and discuss its management. CSP is a rare form of ectopic pregnancy defined by the implantation of the gestational sack into the scar of previous caesarean deliveries (CD). In the past decades, the rates of CSPs have become more frequent due to the increased number of CDs. CSP is associated with high morbidity and mortality for both mother and child and raises ethical and medical concerns. The main role of the physician is the assessment of the risk and proper consultation of the patient. The risk assessment is mainly based on sonographic findings, namely myometrial thickness and distinction between implantation on the scar and in the niche. Depending on the type, the risk of development of a placenta percreta on top of other CSP complications (bleeding fetal death, uterine rupture) is significantly higher. Some studies go as far as defining an in the niche CSP as precursor of percreta accreta spectrum (PAS) implantation. Case Report: We are reporting the case of a 34 year old gravida 4, para 3 presenting with lower abdominal pain and a positive pregnancy test. A transvaginal ultrasound examination showed a viable pregnancy corresponding to 6 4/7 weeks gestational age and fully implanted into the c-section scar and with no visible myometrium thus representing type III CSP (according to Silva et al EJOG, 2023). The patient was informed of the diagnosis and a termination of pregnancy was recommended, either by laparoscopic or hysteroscopic excision or a hysterectomy. The patient opted for a laparoscopic hysterectomy due to finalised family planning. The intra- and postoperative outcome was satisfactory with no complications. Upon extracorporeal hysterotomy, the expected anatomy with a very thin myometrium and a placenta almost adjacent to the serosa was found. Pathology findings showed a placenta percreta. Conclusion: With CSP on the rise but still representing a rare form of intrauterine ectopic pregnancy, knowledge of diagnosis and treatment options become increasingly important. Various efforts have been made to improve risk stratification and thus personalise the approach. Although delivery of a viable child and a healthy mother are described after CSP, expectant or conservative treatment is described to result in serious complications in up to 50%, especially with in the niche CSPs. Therapeutic modalities range from expectant to methotrexate treatment (alone or with aspiration curettage), curettage (with or without simultaneous uterine artery embolization), hysteroscopic approach to laparoscopic excision of the CSP. In our case we found the rare situation that the pregnancy was not desired, so the hysterectomy was one of the options we counselled for. In general, the surgical approach seems to be advantageous.
DO  - 10.1159/000546611
