AB  - Introduction: A uterine niche, also known as a cesarean section scar defect or cesarean-induced isthmocele, is a hypoechoic area or wedge-shaped defect in the lower uterine segment that can result following a cesarean section (CS). The prevalence of niches is increasing due to rising CS rates. This defect is associated with gynecological issues such as abnormal uterine bleeding, dysmenorrhea, and subfertility, with significant clinical implications for subsequent pregnancy including cesarean scar pregnancy, uterine rupture, and placenta accreta spectrum disorder. The aim is to highlight to sonographers the clinical importance of the niche and how to perform an ultrasound assessment of the niche. Method: A literature search using key terms "niche" "c∗sarean section defect", "pregnancy" "ultrasound", "technique" and "protocol" between the years 2011-2025 was performed. Articles were reviewed for the detection and imaging technique of uterine niches in pregnant and non-pregnant women. Results: The uterine niche can be effectively assessed using a transvaginal ultrasound (TVUS) approach. Characterization of the niche including standardized measurements should be part of the imaging protocol in pregnant and non-pregnant women. Conclusion: Uterine niches are a common finding following a CS, with significant implications for women's reproductive health. Accurate imaging and measurements using TVUS are important for the detection and management of niches and for the diagnosis of complications in pregnancy, to improve patient care and outcomes.
DO  - 10.1002/sono.12512
