AB  - Problem: Uterine dendritic cells (uDCs) are pivotal regulators of the immunological environment in the human endometrium, facilitating both immune tolerance and embryo implantation. Despite their recognized roles, the precise ontogeny, molecular heterogeneity, and functional specialization of uDCs across the menstrual cycle and early pregnancy remain largely undefined. Previous studies have suggested that dendritic cells are recruited from peripheral blood, yet emerging evidence indicates the presence of tissue-resident progenitor DCs that contribute to endometrial immune homeostasis. Understanding the distinct subsets of uDCs, their developmental trajectory, and their functional contributions is essential for deciphering immune mechanisms underlying successful implantation and pregnancy maintenance. Additionally, disruptions in uDC function have been implicated in implantation failure, recurrent pregnancy loss, and endometrial disorders, necessitating a comprehensive characterization of theirmolecular profiles and dynamic changes. Method of Study: To systematically investigate uDC heterogeneity and function, we performed single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) on human endometrial tissues collected across 10 menstrual phases and early pregnancy. This transcriptomic dataset was integrated with CITE-seq, enabling simultaneous measurement of protein expression, and multiplex immunohistochemistry (IHC) to validate various dendritic cell subtypes. Computational analysis, including unsupervised clustering, differential gene expression, and pseudotime trajectory inference, was applied to map uDC subsets, infer their lineage differentiation pathways, and define their functional signatures. Additionally, pathway enrichment analysis was conducted to identify key immunological processes regulated by different uDC subtypes. Results: Our integrative analysis revealed seven transcriptionally distinct uDC clusters, including tissue-resident progenitor uDCs (P-uDCs), conventional dendritic cells (cDCs), and peripheral blood-derived uDCs (PB-uDCs). Pseudotime trajectory analysis suggested that progenitor uDCs differentiate into specialized subsets involved in immune regulation, antigen presentation, and tissue remodeling. P-uDCs (CLEC10A+ AXL+ CX3CR1+) exhibited a developmental trajectory toward implantation-supportive cDCs, characterized by upregulation of immune-tolerogenic pathways. In contrast, PB-uDCs (GZMB+ IRF8+) were more prevalent during the proliferative phase, while tissue-resident DCs expanded during the secretory phase, correlating with the window of implantation. Notably, progenitor and implantation-associated DCs exhibited a distinct transcriptional profile enriched for genes associated with cell differentiation, antigen presentation, and immune regulation. These findings suggest a potential role for these DC subsets in shaping the endometrial immune landscape during the implantation window. Conclusion: This study presents the first comprehensive singlecell multi-omics characterization of human uterine dendritic cells, delineating their developmental hierarchy, tissue residency, and functional roles throughout the menstrual cycle and early pregnancy. Our findings challenge the traditional paradigm that all endometrial DCs are recruited from circulation, instead revealing a localized progenitor niche contributing to DC differentiation and immune adaptation. By establishing a detailed molecular framework for uDC function, this study provides critical insights into immune mechanisms underlying implantation success and identifies potential biomarkers or therapeutic targets for recurrent implantation failure and pregnancy complications. Future investigations should focus on functional validation of identified implantation-supportive DC subsets and their interactions with other endometrial immune cells to further refine our understanding of reproductive immunology.
DO  - 10.1111/aji.70088
