AB  - Rising cesarean section (CS) rates lead to an increase in the number of complications. The most common long-term complication after CS is isthmocele, a defect at the site of the cesarean scar. Objective. To study the short-and long-term outcomes of hysteroscopic repair of isthmocele and to justify the feasibility of this method, as well as the conditions and contraindications for its use in this nosology. Patients and methods. This study included 32 patients aged 23 to 44 years with the diagnosis of isthmocele. To assess the severity of the niche, the percentage ratio between the myometrial thickness at the level of the defect (minimum residual myometrial thickness, min RMT) and the maximum myometrial thickness adjacent to the defect (max AMT) was calculated based on the results of transvaginal ultrasound. During hysteroscopy, after confirmation of the diagnosis, the defect was excised along the entire length of the niche using a needle electrode. Results. At 3 months after niche repair, all patients showed a significant increase in min RMT – from 1.3 to 5.8 mm (4.5 times), and the average percentage ratio of max AMT/min RMT decreased from 76.9% (severe, close to extremely severe) to a normal value of 17.2%. The relief of main gynecological symptoms was also noted: abnormal uterine bleeding (88.2%), dysmenorrhea (100%), and dyspareunia (50%). Conclusion. There is no universal method for surgical repair of isthmocele. However, compared to other known techniques, intrauterine metroplasty is relatively simple. It has a lower risk of intra-and postoperative complications and a short recovery period. When the conditions for hysteroscopic surgery are met, its efficacy is not inferior to that of vaginal surgery and laparoscopy.
DO  - 10.20953/1726-1678-2025-6-29-36
