AB  - Objectives: The aim of this study was to compare caesarean section (CS) scar characteristics by transvaginal ultrasound (TVUS) in pregnant women with previous late-stage CS, and to correlate it with risk of subsequent spontaneous preterm birth (sPTB). Methods: In this single-centre prospective cohort study, singleton pregnant women with previous term late-stage CS (2017- 2022,14-24 weeks' gestation) underwent TVUS assessment of cervical length (CL) and CS scar characteristics. sPTB prophylactic interventions were offered for short CL (≤25 mm) and previous history of sPTB/late miscarriage. Scar characteristics and subsequent pregnancy outcomes (sPTB <37 weeks' gestation and CL ≤25 mm) were compared between previous full dilatation CS (FDCS, 10 cm cervical dilatation) and advanced first stage CS (aFSCS, 8-9 cm cervical dilatation). Results: CS scar was visualised in 91.5% (205/224) of FDCS and 87.5% (28/32) of aFSCS groups (p = 0.680); niche was present in 53.2% (109/205) and 35.7% (10/28) (p = 0.126), respectively. Median scar distance to internal os was 5.4 mm, IQR 8.7 mm (FDCS) versus 6.5 mm, IQR 8.8 mm (aFSCS) (p = 0.324). Significantly, no women with aFSCS had a scar below the internal os compared to 15.1% (31/205) in the FDCS group (p = 0.032). sPTB/late miscarriage rates were similar in both groups: FDCS, 3.1% (7/224) versus aFSCS, 3.3% (1/30);13.8% (31/224) FDCS and 12.5% (4/32) aFSCS women shortened CL. Conclusions: CS scar visualisation is similar following previous FDCS or aFSCS. Women with aFSCS were significantly less likely to have a scar within the cervix. Further large scar imaging studies are needed to determine if scar location following aFSCS may predict women at high risk of late miscarriage/ sPTB.
DO  - 10.1111/1471-0528.18006
