AB  - Objectives: In the UK 23% of C-sections are performed as emergencies. 1 Spontaneous preterm birth (sPTB) risk is associated with these,2-4 especially at full dilatation (FDCS).2 The mechanism is complex and multifactorial, but scar location has been implicated. CS scar/niches can be visualised with transvaginal ultrasound (TVUS);5 however, this is only in 70-80% of cases.6,7 MRI advances have facilitated motion correction and generation of 3D anatomical datasets of fetus and maternal anatomy, which may enhance scar identification. The aim of this study is to evaluate the value of 3D reconstructed MRI images in identifying previous FDCS scar/niche. Methods: Datasets were selected from women prospectively recruited to the CRAFT-IMG study. All women had a previous FDCS and fetal MRI at 14-32 weeks' gestation on a 3T imaging system. T2 weighted images were obtained in multiple orthogonal planes and in-house post-processing pipelines generated 3D reconstructions of the cervix/lower uterine segment. The location and characteristics of the scar/niche were noted by three observers - two obstetricians and a radiologist specialising in gynaecological imaging. Intraclass correlation co-efficients (ICCs) were calculated for each feature using SPSS. Results: Forty-nine MRI datasets were suitable for analysis. Good absolute agreement was noted for the presence of scar (ICC 0.738, 95% CI, 0.738-0.921, p < 0.001) and niche (ICC 0.923, 95%CI, 0.866-0.959, p < 0.001) and acceptable for depth of niche from internal os (ICC 0.644, 95% CI, 0.058-0.889, p < 0.019). Conclusions: We have demonstrated that 3D anatomical reconstructions of the cervix/lower segment allow reproducible assessment of the previous scar. Further comparison to TVUS scar visualisation is needed.
DO  - 10.1111/1471-0528.18006
