AB  - Study Objective: To demonstrate a case of uterine remodeling following adhesiolysis. Design: Case report. Setting: Tertiary medical center. Patients or Participants: 29yo G4P2113 with history of Asherman's syndrome and infertility presented with an Isthmocele during fertility optimization prior to embryo transfer. Interventions: The patient underwent a robotic assisted lysis of adhesions and diagnostic hysteroscopy. Key surgical steps illustrated include: • Lysis of omental adhesions • Identification of round ligaments and fallopian tubes • Development of the vesicouterine plane • Backfill bladder • Lysis of abdominal wall adhesions • Diagnostic hysteroscopy using firefly to determine extent of defect • Placement of adhesion barrier. Measurements and Main Results: 3 months postoperative MRI showed release of the uterus from the abdominal wall, substantially improved anterior myometrial thickness and previously seen Isthmocele no longer identified. Conclusion: An obliterated anterior cul-de-sac may lead to extensive myometrial thinning, presenting as an Isthmocele. By performing lysis adhesions and freeing the uterine corpus from the anterior abdominal wall, there's potential for the uterine corpus to remodel and for myometrial thickness to increase.
DO  - 10.1016/j.jmig.2024.09.161
