AB  - Objective: With the increasing rates of cesarean sections, the frequency of cesarean scar defects (CSD) has also risen, becoming more common in clinical practice as spotting and seconder infertility. Materials and Methods: To reduce the incidence of cesarean scar defects, we developed a double layer, parallel-to-incision, non-locking suture technique, and we will compare this technique with the single-layer locking suture technique. Women with early term, term, and postterm pregnancies are being included. Patients will undergo transvaginal ultrasonography three months after the cesarean section to investigate the presence of CSD. Results: In our ongoing study, data from 14 patients have been collected so far, and the result are as follows: The rate of CSD in the study group was found to be statistically significantly lower (28% vs 85.7%, p=0.037). No statistical difference was observed between symptoms and patients’ ages. Although the height of CSD in the study group was found to be statistically significantly lower (1.32mm vs 2.6mm, p=0.015), no difference was observed in the values of length and adjacent myometrial Thickness, and residual myometrial thickness. Discussion and Conclusion: With the increasing rates of cesarean sections, CSD is becoming more prevalent. Recent studies also show a rise in infertility associated with CSD. Therefore, the current statistical data from our study hold promise for significant findings. This study suggests that the double layer, parallel-to-incision, non-locking suture technique may be potentially effective in reducing the development of CSD.
DO  - 10.1016/j.rbmo.2024.104532
