AB  - OBJECTIVE: To determine the effectiveness and safety of hysteroscopic repair of isthmocele with endometrial fluid collection(EFC) in FET cycles and the rate of live birth after the surgery in secondary infertile patients. MATERIALS AND METHODS: A retrospective cohort study that included 26 women diagnosed with endometrial fluid collection (EFC) and isthmocele with a residual myometrium thickness ≥2.5mm. Those patients who attended NOVA IVF fertility clinic, Hyderabad, India, from January 2018 to December 2022 and presented for frozen embryo transfer (FET) were performed hysteroscopic repair of isthmocele. Loop electrode and bipolar storz 22 F resectoscope is used and the procedure is done in a stepwise approach: identification of relevant anatomy; resection of the cephalad and caudad edge of fibrosis; and cauterisation of abnormal endometrium covering the scar base. Details of pregnancy outcome after embryo transfer were recorded. Patients were followed up for 1 year to monitor pregnancy outcome. Complications of surgery were observed, and the rate of live birth were monitored after embryo transfer. RESULTS: Among 26 patients who underwent hysteroscopy isthmocele repair, 18(66.6%) had positive pregnancy. In the remaining cases, 7 (25.92%) patients had negative pregnancy, whereas 1 (3.7%) patient showed incidental finding of simple endometrial hyperplasia. Out of 18 patients with Clinical pregnancy, 11(61.11%) patients continued pregnancy till term, 5(27.7%) patients ended up in biochemical pregnancy and 2 (11.1%) patients had miscarriage. No complications of surgery as bleeding, uterine perforation, infection was noticed. CONCLUSIONS: Hysteroscopic isthmocele repair is safe and effective in treating isthmocele related endometrial fluid collection which in turn results good pregnancy outcome in IVF FET cycles. IMPACT STATEMENT: The data highlights the need for an increased awareness on the part of reproductive medicine specialists towards the potentially adverse impact of EFC due to isthmocele on FET cycles. There is improved live birth rate and no increased incidence of post operative complications, pregnancy failure after the procedure. SUPPORT: no support.
