AB  - Introduction: Breast milk, in addition to being the best food for a child during the first months of life, also provides unique post-uterine immunological protection from mother to child. Early milk (first 5 days postpartum) is called colostrum and consists of essential nutritional and bioactive components, including a large pool of epithelial, progenitor and immune cells. Most of the immune cells are neutrophils, which are part of the innate immune system. Despite remaining in a high percentage along all stages of milk maturation, the biology and function of neutrophils in milk have not been studied to date. Here, we characterize the phenotype and function of colostrum neutrophils, comparing them with those in circulation. Materials and Methods: To conduct this study, we utilized the following techniques: flow cytometry, magnetic cell separation, transmission electron microscopy, proteomics, in vitro phagocytosis assay, and immunocytochemistry. Results: We have observed that neutrophils of colostrum have a phenotype with higher maturation markers and fewer chemotactic markers than blood neutrophils. Images obtained by transmission electron microscopy show essential morphological differences between the two populations. Neutrophils isolated from colostrum show a homogeneous cell surface without pseudopods, an eccentric nucleus and larger cytoplasmic vesicles, suggesting a different function in this population. In addition, proteomic analysis results suggest that colostrum neutrophils play a role in humoral acquired immunity, while blood neutrophils focus on pathogen protection. Conclusions: Breast milk contains a subpopulation of neutrophils with unique functions that could play a crucial role in the baby's immune protection during the first few months of life.
