AB  - Introduction: Cesarean deliveries Cesarean deliveries have become increasingly common in both developed and developing countries. The exact incidence of isthmocele is unknown, but it’s thought to be up to 61% after primary cesarean and up to 100% after tertiary cesarean. Isthmoceles can cause post-menstrual spotting due to accumulation of blood at the site of the defect, chronic pelvic pain, vaginal discharge, dyspareunia, and secondary infertility. Patients with an isthmocele are at higher risk of cesarean scar pregnancy, placenta accreta spectrum, and uterine scar dehiscence. Large defects are associated with an increase in scar dehiscence and uterine rupture in a subsequent pregnancy. They are diagnosed by transvaginal ultrasound, saline ultrasound, hysterosalpingogram, hysteroscopy, or MRI. Imaging should be completed on days 6-12, during the proliferative phase right after the menstrual cycle. Management if symptomatic and desiring fertility is by laparoscopic repair with or without robot use, hysteroscopic repair, or vaginal repair. Hysteroscopic repair can be completed if there is over 3mm of myometrium remaining. Objective: The objectives of this video are to present the case of a 36 year old G6P3033 undergoing workup for infertility. The patient had been trying to conceive for 6 months without success. Saline ultrasound revealed a large isthmocele with less than 3mm of myometrium remaining. Robotic-assisted repair of the isthmocele was completed with a two layer closure. Hysteroscopy confirmed intact closure. Methods: Video was filmed during surgical intervention for patient presented. Results: Isthmocele was repaired in two layers. Closure confirmed to be intact on hysteroscopic evaluation after the case. Conclusions: In conclusion, isthmocele is not an uncommon complication of cesarean section and can be a cause of infertility and pelvic pain. This case demonstrates successful excision of isthmocele and repair with two layer closure. Repair was confirmed to be intact with hysteroscopy post-procedure. SIS will be performed 3 months post-operatively to confirm appropriate healing, at which time the patient will be able to proceed with attempting conception. Disclosure: No.
DO  - 10.1016/j.ajog.2024.02.263
