AB  - Introduction: The uterine cesarean section scar defect causes postmenstrual uterine bleeding. The niche, particularly with a thin residual myometrium, is a risk factor of uterine rupture at the end of pregnancy and abnormal implantation of the GS or placenta in the area of the uterine scar. IUDs are an effective method of contraception and those with levonorgestrel (IUD-LND) also reduce menstrual bleeding and consequently postmenstrual bleeding. Nevertheless, the presence of a uterine scar defect is a risk factor for the IUD to dislocate into the diverticulum. The aim of this work is to present the results of an attempt of reposition the IUD back to the uterine cavity. Methods: IUD displacement into the scar defect was observed in 5 patients (3 with IUD-Cu, 2 with IUD-LNG). The IUD was repositioned into the uterine cavity and USG-TV confirmed the correct localization. Follow up was performed at 1 month and 6 months after the procedure, or if abnormal uterine bleeding had occurred. Results: At the end of a 6-month observation period all (100%) patients had the IUD dislocated into the myometrial scar defect: in 3 patients with symptoms of AUB (heavy and/or prolonged) after 2 weeks to 3 months, in 2 patients with subtle symptoms during a scheduled check-up. In 3 patients, the IUD was repositioned to the cavity again, but after about 1-1.5 months it relocated to the diverticulum. Conclusion: A single displacement of the IUD to the uterine scar cesarean section defect will result in its recurrent dislocation. A disorder of the architecture of the uterine anterior wall is a probable cause of a disturbance in the distribution of forces during uterine contractions, however other factors must also be considered.
DO  - 10.1016/j.ejogrb.2023.08.083
