AB  - With the increase in the number of cesarean sections attention focused on the development and consequences of niche (cesarean scar defect-CSD) visible on transvaginal ultrasound examination (TVUS) as an indentation in the uterine scar. Most publications showed a decreasing rate of CSD with time after cesarean section (CS), however there were few analyses of prognostic value of TVUS in late puerperium in prediction of niche in TVUS 3 months and later after CS. The aim of this study was to evaluate the predictive value of TVUS at 5–8-weeks after CS (TVUS-1), in prediction of niche in TVUS 12–16-weeks after surgery (TVUS-2). The study group consisted of 116 women who underwent CS followed by TVUS-1 and TVUS-2 in which presence of niche was noted, defined as indentation with the depth ≥ 2 mm. Residual-myometrial-thickness (RMT), depth, length, width of niche were measured and tested to evaluate predictive value of the TVUS-1 using logistic regression, analysis of the area under the ROC curve (AUC), sensitivity, specificity, PPV, NPV. The incidence of niche in TVUS-1 and TVUS-2 was 69.0% and 37.1% respectively; p < 0.001. Logistic regression showed that presence of CSD in TVUS-1 significantly increased risk of niche in TVUS-2 (OR13.41 (2.92–61.68); p < 0.001), however presence of CSD in TVUS-1, without analysis of its dimensions, showed high sensitivity (95.4%) and NPV (94.4%) with low specificity (46.6% ) and PPV (51.2%) in prediction of niche in TVUS-2. The highest values of AUC (0.801) and sensitivity (93.0%) for prediction of niche in TVUS-2 were found if one of the three dimensions of the CSD in TVUS-1 exceeded a set threshold (depth ≥ 4.5 mm; length ≥ 7.0 mm; width 7.5 mm). TVUS in late puerperium showed potential in prediction of niche in TVUS 3-4 months after CS.
DO  - 10.1016/j.ejogrb.2023.08.351
