AB  - Introduction and aims of the study: Due to the increasing incidence of Caesarean Section (CS) worldwide, uterine scar niche presents a rising problem. It can cause chronic pelvic pain, dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia and abnormal uterine bleeding. Possible complications are caesarean scar ectopic pregnancy, placenta previa, abnormal placenta attachment and uterine rupture, all of which are associated with major maternal morbidity and mortality. We report a case of uterine scar niche laparoscopic repair. Methods: This case concerns a 23-year-old patient with 3 previous CS, the last 1,5 years ago. She complained of heavy menstrual bleeding and prolonged spotting before and after menstruation, lasting up to 2 weeks. Transvaginal sonography showed a V-shaped anterior uterine wall defect in the caesarean scar region with residual myometrial thickness of 2,6 mm. Endometrium penetrated into the scar defect. The patient was prescribed combined oral contraception to minimise bleedings and was referred to laparoscopic uterine niche correction in 2 months. Uterine scar region was excised laparoscopically using ultrasound knife and monopolar. The wound was sutured in 2 layers: first layer with single resorbable sutures, second with running V-loc suture. After that wound was covered with an absorbable adhesion barrier (Interceed). Results: Follow-up period continued for 8 months. 4 months after the operation the patient had regular 5-day-long menstruations with minimal bleeding. Transvaginal sonography showed uniform anterior uterine wall with myometrial thickness of 6,6 mm and thin uniform endometrium 4,8 mm. Conclusion: Such long-term complications of CS as uterine scar niche can cause major problems for young fertile women. If a patient has complaints and/or further reproductive plans, niche can be treated laparoscopically or hysteroscopically. To prevent niche formation, CS percentage should be well-controlled and kept at minimal level. Correct CS surgical technique is also extremely important.
DO  - 10.1016/j.ejogrb.2023.08.104
