AB  - Introduction: Laparoscopy is one of the approaches of repairing the cesarean section diverticulum, besides laparotomy and the transvaginal access. The endoscopic method provides better access to the operated site, high precision of reconstruction and reduced traumatization of abdominal wall. However, this method is limited by the demanding laparoscopic suturing. The aim of the study was to assess early perioperative complications after laparoscopic uterine cesarean scar repair. Methods: The analysis included 127 patients undergoing laparoscopic surgery due to caesarean section defect, in whom a residual muscle thickness of <2.5 mm was found during qualification (hystersonography), and who declared reproductive plans. Complications were assessed according to the Clavien-Dindo scale, yet for the overall incidence assessment those classified at grade 2 or more were analyzed. Results: Grade 4 early complications were reported in 3.9% of patients: in 2 iatrogenic bladder injury (1.6% of operated patients), in 2 (1.6%) bleeding from the uterine vessel branches, in 1 (0.8%) incision of the anterior vaginal wall during the dissection of the bladder from the uterus and the anterior wall of the vagina. Early stage 2 complications were reported in 12.6% of patients: 2 (1.6%) severe lower abdominal pain with symptoms of incipient surgical site infection, 1 (0.8%) infection of the navel trocar wound, 1 (1.6%) hematoma between the uterus and bladder, 12 (9.4%) had moderate anemia requiring pharmacotherapy (only 2 had a normal hemoglobin value at baseline). There was no conversion to laparotomy. 7 days after the procedure there was no early scar diverticulum. Conclusions: The risk of surgery mainly results from complications of the operated site – especially adherence of the bladder with the anterior wall of the uterus and is comparable to TLH. The risk of infection is lower than in laparotomy procedures.
DO  - 10.1016/j.ejogrb.2023.08.165
