AB  - Objective: In this work, we employ microporous scaffolds as a synthetic immunological niche to remotely capture the longitudinal immune domain of healthy and preeclamptic-like, miscarriage-prone rodent pregnancies with greater specificity than blood (Fig 1A). Study Design: Microporous poly(ε-caprolactone) (PCL) scaffolds were implanted subcutaneously in C57BL/6 female mice prior to cohabitation with BALB/c (allogeneic) or C57BL/6 (syngeneic) males (FIg 1B). For preeclamptic-like pregnancies, CBA females were bred with DBA/2 males. Scaffolds were serially explanted prior to cohabitation and on embryonic days (E) 5.5, 10.5, and 15.5, along with age-matched non-breeders. Blood, spleen, uterus, placentae, and implanted scaffold cells were processed for flow cytometry, T cell activation, and trophoblast migration. Scaffolds were also sequenced for differential gene expression and pathway enrichment. Results: In both healthy and preeclamptic-like pregnancies, the scaffold implant recapitulates tissue-resident immune cell phenotypes (including NK cell and macrophage phenotypes) and the immune microenvironment of the uterus and placentae with greater tissue specificity than found in circulation (Fig 1D, E). In addition to the innate immune cell phenotypes in the scaffold, gene expression in the scaffold can be used to distinguish between gestational stages and states (Fig 1C). Through an Elastic Net regression, we identified 8 genes that differentiate between allogeneic-bred and syngeneic-bred mice at embryonic day 5.5 (E5.5), prior to fetal or placental organogenesis, to construct a predictive biomarker panel for the risk of preeclampsia. Lastly, scaffolds capture a population of fetal cells identified in the placentae at E15.5. Conclusion: We have developed scaffold implants to remotely monitor immunological markers of prenatal progression which recapitulate aspects of the uterine microenvironment. Scaffold gene expression can differentiate the prenatal immune state, and scaffolds capture a population of fetal cells from circulation. These scaffolds act as a minimally invasive prenatal monitoring tool. [Formula presented]
