AB  - A caesarean scar ectopic may create havoc as making a diagnosis of scar ectopic is very difficult. The increasing rates of caesarean deliveries has lead to the emergence of problems in both obstetrics (such as placenta previa, Placenta accrete, scar ectopic and scar rupture in subsequent pregnancies) and gynecological complications (such as AUB, Pelvic pain, Caesarean niche and secondary infertility). Caesarean scar ectopic is a rare form of ectopic pregnancy with its incidence being 0.05% of all pregnancies. Management of a peculiar case of caesarean scar ectopic pregnancy has been discussed below.
