AB  - OBJECTIVE: To describe the surgical management and postoperative fertility outcomes in a patient with retained products of conception (RPOC) trapped within a uterine isthmocele after single-embryo transfer, with successful subsequent spontaneous pregnancy. MATERIALS AND METHODS: This was an IRB exempt study. Systematic literature review found no other similar case reports describing post frozen embryo transfer (FET) RPOC in an isthmocele with postoperative fertility follow up. The patient is a 31-year-old G1P1001 with a history of peritoneal factor infertility who was refractory to treatment with letrozole and multiple rounds of intrauterine insemination. Primary pregnancy was achieved via in-vitro fertilization (IVF) with subsequent single FET and delivered by low transverse cesarean section for breech presentation. For her second pregnancy, single FET was performed. At 6 gestational weeks, ultrasound revealed an intrauterine pregnancy (IUP) with subchorionic hematoma, subsequently resulting in missed abortion at 7 weeks. She opted for observational management, and after 2 weeks she was treated with misoprostol for failure to pass POC. With downtrending bHCG levels, she had persistent vaginal bleeding without passage of POC. Pelvic ultrasound revealed a thin endometrial stripe with blood in the cavity and a possible clot or mass. This was confirmed via MRI, finding a 5.4 x 6.0 x 5.8 cm heterogeneous mass within an isthmocele (cesarean scar defect) with continuity to the endometrial canal, suggestive of RPOC. Due to persistent bleeding and noted vascular aspects of the RPOC, methotrexate was administered to reduce the vascularity. The avascular RPOC still did not pass despite her bHCG levels falling to 0. With consideration for preserving fertility, the patient opted for minimally invasive evacuation of POC and isthmocele repair. Retained POCs were removed hysteroscopically under laparoscopic guidance, and the isthmocele was excised and repaired in two layers robotically with minimal blood loss. The patient was discharged home the same day with an uncomplicated recovery. RESULTS: Thirteen weeks after surgical repair, she underwent saline infusion sonography which revealed a normal uterine cavity and a post-surgical scar that was within normal limits. Patient conceived spontaneously in the same cycle of her saline-infusion sonogram, resulting in a viable IUP. Antenatal course was slightly complicated by a small subchorionic hemorrhage at 7 weeks, which resolved spontaneously by 10 weeks without affecting the pregnancy. Patient underwent a repeat low transverse cesarean section at 37 weeks to a healthy newborn, discharged on postoperative day 2 without complications. CONCLUSIONS: Retained POC within an isthmocele after FET can be managed in a minimally-invasive manner with successful postoperative pregnancy outcomes. IMPACT STATEMENT: There have been no previous reports of successful combined excision of RPOC within an isthmocele and isthmoplasty after frozen embryo transfer with documented postoperative spontaneous pregnancy. This is a unique case that has both surgical and reproductive significance. SUPPORT: This research received no specific grant from any funding agency in the public, commercial, or not-for-profit sectors.
