AB  - OBJECTIVE: To describe a minimally invasive approach to repair of an isthmocele in an unusual location. METHODOLOGY: 31-year-old G6P3033 patient with history of three prior cesarean deliveries presented with irregular vaginal bleeding and secondary unexplained infertility. An isthmocele in the lateral uterine wall was missed on transvaginal ultrasound but identified on MRI. The isthmocele was repaired in a robotic-assisted laparoscopic procedure using the following key surgical techniques: 1. Retroperitoneal dissection to facilitate adequate ureterolysis and identification of the ascending branch of the uterine artery and lateral uterine wall isthmocele 2. Pre and post hysteroscopy to visualize defect and subsequent repair 3. Injection of dilute vasopressin to minimize bleeding prior to repair 4. Chromopertubation, placement of a uterine manipulator or sound, or both to confirm correct location prior to repair CONCLUSIONS: Uterine isthmocele, while typically located in the anterior lower uterine segment, may be found in more unusual locations. Lateral uterine wall isthmocele can be repaired with many of the same key surgical techniques as anterior isthmocele; however, comfort with retroperitoneal dissection, ureterolysis, and identification of the ascending branch of the uterine artery is paramount for the safety of these structures during surgical repair. IMPACT STATEMENT: This case report highlights that isthmocele in unusual locations can still cause menstrual irregularity and impact fertility. Reproductive surgeons should feel comfortable with retroperitoneal dissection when performing similar procedures to ensure safety and success. SUPPORT: There was no financial support for the creation of this video. REFERENCES: Kremer TG Ghiorzi IB Dibi RP. Isthmocele: an overview of diagnosis and treatment. Rev Assoc Med Bras (1992). 2019 Jun 3;65(5):714-721. PMID: Dasari P Jha N Thiyagarajan C. Isthmocele of lateral wall of uterus encountered at Suction evacuation- A maternal near-miss. J Obstet Gynecol Res 2020;7(4):604-607
