AB  - OBJECTIVE: Determine the pregnancy rate and live birth outcomes in women with secondary infertility after laparoscopic repair of uterine isthmocele. MATERIALS AND METHODS: Retrospective review of a prospectively maintained database of all women with secondary infertility desiring pregnancy who underwent laparoscopic isthmocele repair from January 1, 2015 until December 31, 2021. Descriptive statistics were used to characterize the patient population including age, BMI, ethnicity, and number of cesarean sections prior to isthmocele repair. Pre-operative and post-operative isthmocele repair findings including myometrial thickness in the cesarean scar defect both before and after repair, absence or presence of fluid and subsequent pregnancy outcomes were also characterized. RESULTS: The study sample included 67 patients with a mean age at time of surgery of 36± 3.3 years old. 61% of the patients were white with an average BMI of 24.3 (range: 17.4-48.3). During isthmocele repair, additional pathology was identified in nearly two-thirds (64%) of patients, most commonly being adhesions (31%) and endometriosis (24%). In the 46 patients with available imaging and remaining myometrial thickness measurements pre and post operatively, the median change in remaining myometrial thickness was 3.9mm (range 0-11mm). Pregnancy was achieved by 52 patients (78%) and of those 45 (67%) delivered a viable infant. CONCLUSIONS: The incidence of cesarean section continues to grow both in the United States and internationally. As the incidence grows so do various sequelae resulting from cesarean section, one of the most notable being the uterine isthmocele. A uterine isthmocele is a pouch-like defect on the anterior wall of the uterine isthmus at the site of a previous cesarean section scar. It is believed that secondary infertility can result from accumulation of blood or mucus in the defect leading to inflammation and consequent prevention of entrance of sperm into the uterus or embryo implantation. Currently there is no consensus in the literature as to the optimal treatment for patients with secondary infertility and uterine isthmocele who desire subsequent pregnancy. Our study demonstrates improved pregnancy rates and live birth outcomes outcomes in patients with secondary infertility after laparoscopic repair of isthmocele and should be considered as primary treatment in women with isthmocele measuring 3mm or less desiring future fertility. IMPACT STATEMENT: Women with secondary infertility found to have an isthmocele measuring 3mm or less, or found to have fluid in the isthmocele, have improved pregnancy rates and live birth outcomes after laparoscopic repair of the isthmocele. REFERENCES: Donnez O, et al. Gynecological and obstetrical outcomes after laparoscopic repair of a cesarean scar defect in a series of 38 women. Fertil Steril. 2017 Jan He Y, et al. Four Surgical Strategies for the Treatment of Cesarean Scar Defect: A Systematic Review and Network Meta-analysis. J Minim Invasive Gynecol. Mar-Apr 2020
DO  - 10.1016/j.fertnstert.2023.08.528
