AB  - Objective: Retrospective studies have highlighted spontaneous preterm birth (sPTB) risk following full dilatation caesarean section (FDCS) (1-6). MRI demonstrates evidence of a sphincter within the internal cervical os (7) and injury near/within the proximal cervix may be implicated (8). We assessed anatomical cervical features in women with a previous term FDCS using ultrasound (TVUS) and MRI. Methods: 29 pregnant women with a previous FDCS were prospectively recruited and underwent TVUS and MRI at 16-28 weeks observing cervical length (CL), caesarean scar and/or niche dimensions and distance to internal cervical os. We used a TVUS protocol with good intra/inter-observer variability (9). T2 weighted 3-Tesla MRIs were obtained in multiple orthogonal planes and reconstructed using in-house developed pipelines. Chi-square and Bland-Altman tests compared imaging findings. Results: Of the TVUS niche cases there was MRI niche agreement in 9/14 (64%) (p = <0.001) (Table 1). 11/15 (73%) cases had no visualised niche by either modality. 4/15 (27%) cases had niche visualised by MRI only. 9/12 niches were located below at or below the cervical os. For niche level see Table 2. There was good agreement in all parameters except niche length with TVUS and MRI (Table 3). There was no association between presence of defect and preterm birth rate in our small sample. Conclusion: There was agreement between parameters measured using both modalities. It is difficult to comment on accuracy of defect detection as there is no defined gold standard. A larger study examining correlation between defects, modality and sPTB is warranted.
DO  - 10.1111/1471-0528.17419
