AB  - Objective: To evaluate caesarean section (CS) scar characteristics by transvaginal ultrasound (TVUS) in pregnant women with previous full dilatation caesarean section (FDCS) to develop multiparameter screening models for prediction of spontaneous preterm birth (SPTB). Methods: In this single centre prospective cohort study, singleton pregnant women with previous term FDCS (2017 2021, 14 24 weeks' gestation) underwent serial TVUS assessment of cervical length (CL), FDCS scar/niche characteristics. FDCS scar distance to internal os was measured, with internal os identified using uterine artery colour Doppler. SPTB prophylactic interventions were offered for CL ? 25 mm or in women with previous history of SPTB/late miscarriage after FDCS. Multivariable logistic regression analysis was used to develop multiparameter models. Results: FDCS scar was visualised in 220/243 (90.5%) women. SPTB rate was 4.1% (10/243); 12.8% (31/243) of women had short CL. History (n = 4) or ultrasound (n = 19) indicated cerclage was performed in 23/243 (9.5%) women; two delivered preterm. Predictive models for short CL using scar distance alone, or scar distance with niche parameters, had AUC of 0.79 (95% CI, 0.71 0.87) and 0.81 (95% CI, 0.73 0.89), respectively, with 73% detection rate (DR) at 25% false-positive rate (FPR). Predictive models for SPTB based on scar distance had AUC of 0.73 (95% CI, 0.57 0.89) with 60% DR (25% FPR). SPTB was more likely when the CS scar was <5 mm above or below the internal os (aOR 6.87, 95% CI, 1.34 58, p = 0.035). Conclusion: In pregnancies after FDCS, multiparameter screening models with FDCS scar characteristics can predict women at increased risk of short CL and SPTB.
DO  - 10.1111/1471-0528.17419
