AB  - Objective: Demonstrate that robotic assisted repair of a Cesarean scar defect (isthmocele) is safe and effective for future fertility Description: This is a step-wise demonstration of hysteroscopic findings of a Cesarean scar defect and subsequent repair of this defect robotically. A patient presents for secondary infertility and abnormal uterine bleeding with a history of three prior Cesarean sections, and is diagnosed with a large isthmocele on ultrasound. Patient desired future fertility, and elected for robotic assisted laparoscopic repair of this isthmocele. The patient was then found to be pregnant spontaneously at her six week post-operative visit. She had an uncomplicated prenatal course and was ultimately delivered via repeat Cesarean section at 37 weeks gestation without complications. Conclusion: Cesarean scar defect (isthmocele) is a significant complication of Cesarean delivery, and is associated with secondary infertility. Here, we confirm isthmocele diagnosis on hysteroscopy and laparoscopy. Robotic assisted laparoscopic repair of isthmocele is considered a safe and effective procedure as patient had a scheduled repeat Cesarean section at 37 weeks gestation without complications.
DO  - 10.1016/j.ajog.2022.12.274
