AB  - Introduction: Uterine rupture is an intrinsically biomechanical process, in which excessive forces cause tissue disruption. Uterine scar increases rupture risk by an order of magnitude and is a growing obstetrical challenge, risking both fetal and maternal morbidity and mortality. To examine the biomechanics of prior C-section scar, stress concentrations were modeled for different scar geometries and compared with clinical outcomes. Methods: The model uterus geometry was based on MRI images of an at-term pregnant patient (week 38 gestation). Geometries of the abdomen, uterus, cervix, and a C-section scar were modeled in Solidworks and imported into Hypermesh for discretization into elements (Fig. 1A). Assuming a nearly incompressible (Poisson's ratio value of 0.48) neo-Hookean elastic material for all components, fnite element simulations were completed in FEBio Studio with an applied intrauterine pressure of 2.6 kPa (Fig. 1A). The walls of the abdomen were fxed in the X, Y and Z directions as shown in Fig.1A. A Young's modulus (E) of 100 kPa was used for the abdomen and uterus, and the scar tissue stifness was varied about this value (E values of 50kPa, 100 kPa or 200 kPa). Quantitative heat maps of von Mises (representative 3D) stress were generated to compare the tissue stress in sagittal and transverse C-section scars and surrounding tissue. Results: The von Mises stress on the C-section scar increased with increased stifness of the scar tissue (Fig. 1B). The magnitude of the von Mises stress was greater for all sagittal scars compared to the respective transverse scar with the same Young's modulus (Fig. 1B). Surprisingly, regions with high stress in the transverse scar models were in the uterine tissue at the scar edges, not just in the scar tissue itself (Fig. 1B). Control models where the scar properties were matched to surrounding tissue properties validated the boundary conditions and demonstrated the expected null result. Conclusion: Computed stress distributions confrmed an increased risk of uterine rupture in sagittal (classical) scars compared with low transverse scars, consistent with clinical observations. Experimental measurements of uterine scar tissue are needed to refne these models further. Regardless, fnite element modeling is a powerful tool for patient-Specific surgical planning. Results here indicate the potential for optimizing transverse scar placement and size. Ongoing studies are examining individual layers of the uterine wall to examine the effects of isthmocele.
DO  - 10.1007/s43032-023-01211-1
