AB  - Objective: To find the predictors of postmenstrual spotting based on MRI assessment of cesarean scar niche Materials and Methods: A secondary analysis of a cohort study included women diagnosed with cesarean scar niche by transvaginal sonography 6-12 months after the cesarean section (CS). MRI was done for evaluation of the shape, length, width, depth, volume of the niche and the minimal scar thickness measurement. The relative change percentage in myometrial thickness in relation to the posterior wall was assessed. Results: We found that the scar depth (OR= 3.47, 95%CI= 2.33-7.98, p<0.001), scar width (OR= 2.5, 95%CI= 2.11-5.55, p<0.001), and scar volume (OR= 7, 95%CI= 3.40-11.40, p<0.001) were significant predictors of postmenstrual spotting in women with niche. On the other hand, scar length (OR= 0.76, 95%CI= 0.33-3.45, p=0.12), minimal scar thickness (OR= 1.03, 95%CI= 0.40-2.11, p=0.11) and the relative change percentage (OR= 2.11, 95%CI= 0.23-3.11, p=0.09) were not predictors. ROC curve analyses found at cut off >7.4 mm, scar depth had 81% sensitivity and 97% specificity for prediction of post-menstrual spotting with overall accuracy was 88.7%, while scar width at cut off > 12.8 mm, had 71% sensitivity and 97% specificity for prediction of post-menstrual spotting with overall accuracy was 83.3%. Scar volume at cut off > 0.15 cm3 had 97% sensitivity and 100% specificity for prediction of post-menstrual spotting with overall accuracy was 98.4%. Conclusions: Cesarean scar niche volume, depth, and width assessed by MRI are significant predictors for postmenstrual spotting in women with cesarean scar niche. Impact Statement: MRI assessment of cesarean scar niche volume, depth, and width proved to be significant predictors for postmenstrual spotting in women with cesarean scar niche.
DO  - 10.1016/j.fertnstert.2021.07.883
