AB  - OBJECTIVE: We present a unique case of diffuse peritoneal endometriosis following cesarean section, involving the cesarean scar isthmocele and associated with secondary infertility, failed embryo transfer, and progressive pelvic pain. METHODOLOGY: The case presented is of a 44-year-old, G6P3032, with three prior cesarean deliveries and a laparoscopic appendectomy, in none of which, endometriosis was visualized. Her symptoms started 14 years ago, following her third cesarean section, as dysmenorrhea and dyspareunia that progressed to chronic pelvic pain, neuropathic left leg pain, urinary symptoms, and recurrent failed embryo transfers. The initial evaluation was performed due to her desire for pregnancy, with a transvaginal ultrasound and MRI that demonstrated a sharply retroverted uterus and a prominent, fluid filled, cesarean scar defect. A combined hysteroscopic and laparoscopic approach was planned. Indocyanine green dye was used to identify the bladder borders and methylene blue was added to the hysteroscopy irrigation solution to create contrast. After vasopressin injection, we performed a laparoscopic wide excision of the isthmocele margins, leading to thick opposing walls. A three-layer closure was then performed to maintain the integrity of the uterus and its obstetric compatibility. First, a sub-mucosal purse-string suture was placed, connecting the fresh layers at each side of the defect. Then, two 'stay sutures' were placed in the angles, followed by five transmural interrupted sutures, in a cephalocaudal manner, connecting the proximal and distal edges of the defect. A third suture layer was then introduced to reinforce the suture line by incorporating the uterine serosa. We then completed endometriosis excision of all apparent lesions. We used the AquaBlue contrast technique (ABCt™) that includes hydrodistention of the retroperitoneum with diluted methylene blue. In the pathological assessment, multiple foci of endometriosis were identified within the isthmocele membrane, clearly differentiated from intrauterine endometrial tissue. Additionally, all seven excised peritoneal specimens contained peritoneal endometriosis. Two weeks following the procedure, transvaginal sonographic scan confirmed thick anterior uterine wall, and the patient reported only minor discomfort with almost complete resolution of her symptoms. CONCLUSIONS: This case demonstrates endometriosis within the isthmocele membrane, with concomitant symptomatic peritoneal endometriosis. We propose a laparoscopic isthmocele excision technique with three-layer reconstruction, and peritoneal endometriosis excision using methylene blue contrast. Surgical treatment of isthmocele can be offered to symptomatic patients, or asymptomatic patients who desires future pregnancy. IMPACT STATEMENT: We suggest a possible link between isthmocele and endometriosis. We emphasize the need for wide excision of the isthmocele margins and maintaining clean borders, given the possibility of endometriosis within the isthmocele, which might be a cause or a contributor to the tissue weakness and isthmocele formation.
