AB  - Background: Following a cesarean section, a discontinuity or diverticulum, in the endometrium and myometrium at the anterior lower uterine segment may develop1. This diverticulum is referred to as a “cesarean scar defect” (CSD). These defects can present with a number of symptoms, including abnormal uterine bleeding, dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, cesarean scar ectopic pregnancies, or secondary infertility2. Some patients are also asymptomatic. CSDs are visualized as a fluid-filled, anechoic, triangular region, often at the isthmus, which is detected by imaging during workup for these symptoms. Currently, there is limited data on the management of cesarean scar defects, although laparoscopic resection and repair is often performed with a residual myometrial thickness (RMT) <3mm at the site of the defect and hysteroscopic resection can be considered when the RMT is >3mm. Data on fertility and pregnancy outcomes is also sparse following treatment. A recent systematic review showed an 87-100% improvement in abnormal uterine bleeding, a 97-100% improvement in pain symptoms, and a pregnancy rate of 92%3. Objective: Our aim was to examine treatment methods, symptom improvement, and pregnancy outcomes in a cohort of women diagnosed and treated for a CSD in the context of secondary infertility. Materials and Methods: This is a retrospective cohort study. A chart review was completed for patients diagnosed with and treated for a CSD between 2008 and 2019 at a tertiary care centre by one of three gynecologists with expertise in CSDs. Results: 26 patients were identified as experiencing secondary infertility with diagnosis of a CSD. The mean age was 36.4 years. 23 patients had 1 prior caesarean section and 3 patients had 2 prior caesarean sections. Of note, 3 patients had previously undergone treatment for a caesarean scar ectopic pregnancy. Of the 26 patients, 20 underwent surgical management; 12 in the form of hysteroscopic ablation or resection, and 8 with laparoscopic resection and repair. 6 patients had no treatment or are still waiting for management at this time. Following surgery, 11 of the 20 patients (55%) were able to successfully conceive at least once. 8 of the 11 patients were from the hysteroscopy group (66% pregnancy rate) and there were 10 pregnancies, which all resulted in live births at term. In the laparoscopy group, there were 3 pregnancies (37.5% pregnancy rate); 2 were term live births, and 1 was a preterm live birth at 26 weeks with preterm premature rupture of membranes (PPROM) and severe intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR). CONCLUSIONS: This study suggests that appropriate treatment (based on the residual myometrial thickness) can improve pregnancy rates in patients with secondary infertility in the context of a confirmed caesarean scar defect. There are limitations given the retrospective design of this study, and a prospective study would be needed to confirm these findings. Financial Support: None References:: 1. Allornuvor GF, Xue M, Zhu X, Xu D. The definition, aetiology, presentation, diagnosis and management of previous caesarean scar defects. J Obstet Gynaecol 2013; 33(8): 759-63. 2. Setubal A, Alves J, Osorio F, et al. Treatment for Uterine Isthmocele, A Pouchlike Defect at the Site of a Cesarean Section Scar. J Minim Invasive Gynecol 2018; 25(1): 38-46. 3. Bij de Vaate AJ, van der Voet LF, Naji O, et al. Prevalence, potential risk factors for development and symptoms related to the presence of uterine niches following Cesarean section: systematic review. Ultrasound Obstet Gynecol 2014; 43(4): 372-82.
DO  - 10.1016/j.fertnstert.2021.05.010
