AB  - Objective: The objective of this video is to review the principles of cervicovaginal fistula repair through a vaginal approach for patients interested in future fertility. Description: We detail the case of a 33-year-old patient with a history of cesarean section two years prior complicated by hysterotomy extension requiring complex repair and significant postpartum hemorrhage. She presented to an outside provider with inability to conceive and dyspareunia since her cesarean section. Evaluation included diagnostic hysteroscopy, which demonstrated a communication between the endocervix and the vagina. Upon referral to our institution, MRI demonstrated a fistulous tract between the anterior vaginal fornix and the endocervix 0.5 cm caudal to the cesarean section scar. Given her desire for future fertility, inability to conceive, and dyspareunia, she was offered fistula repair under anesthesia. We emphasize principles of vaginal surgical repair including: (1) reliable preoperative imaging, (2) hysteroscopic visualization of the defect, (3) bladder mobilization and development of the vesicovaginal space, (4) delineation of the borders of the fistulous tract, (5) debridement and defect excision, (6) multi-layer closure parallel to the length of the cervix, and (7) confirmation of cervical patency. Conclusion: These strategies apply to repair of other cesarean scar defect types, including isthmocele, niche, and uteroperitoneal fistula, and allow for a minimally invasive approach for patients desiring future fertility.
DO  - 10.1016/j.ajog.2021.04.176
