AB  - Objectives: We present the clinical course of a multiparous woman diagnosed with a large cesarean scar defect (CSD)who underwent hysterectomy. We also review the diagnosis of isthmocele, our surgical management, and the unexpected pathology findings of epithelioid trophoblastic tumor (ETT). Materials and Methods: A 42-year-old female with history of four prior cesarean sections presented 18 months postpartum with secondary amenorrhea. She was diagnosed with a large lower uterine segment isthmocele on transvaginal ultrasonography. The patient had completed childbearing and opted for surgical management via hysterectomy. She underwent total laparoscopic hysterectomy. Surgery was notable for significant adhesions from the lower uterine segment to the bladder, requiring dissection via an inferior and lateral approach after ligation of the uterine vessels. Results: Pathologic examination revealed an epithelioid trophoblastic neoplasm. The tumor was described as 2.5 cm x 1 cm, confined to the uterus, and diffusely infiltrating the lower uterine segment without invasion into the surrounding tissue. Negative margins were identified. The patient underwent consultation with Gynecology Oncology who recommended surveillance with serial beta-human chorionic gonadotropin and imaging. Conclusion: The clinical presentation and diagnosis of CSD have been thoroughly described. Patients commonly present with postmenstrual spotting, and diagnosis can be made via transvaginal ultrasound. In women who have completed childbearing, hysterectomy is a reasonable treatment option. Many approaches to repair of CSD have been proposed, however approach to hysterectomy in patients with large isthmoceles has not been well documented. Moreover, while desmoid tumors have been described as an extremely rare finding within a CSD, we could only find a single case report in which an ETT was found in someone with a CSD. We reviewed the literature surrounding CSDs in the context of our patient’s clinical course. We concluded that: 1) high suspicion of CSD is warranted in a patient with unexplained amenorrhea and prior cesareans; 2) during hysterectomy, performing adhesiolysis by starting at the vesicocervical tissue and progressing superiorly may be helpful in the case of significant adhesive disease; and 3) is possible that ETT is related to CSD but further research is needed to examine this relationship. [Formula presented] [Formula presented] [Formula presented]
DO  - 10.1016/j.ajog.2021.04.140
