AB  - Background A pregnancy in the presence of a scar defect, also called a niche, delays diagnosis and hence treatment. Diagnosis of scar pregnancy can be made either with ultrasound or MRI and diagnostic criteria are the same. A niche is defined as a defect in the myometrium, at the site of the scar of a caesarean section, of at least 2 mm. The defect is classified as large when it is more than 50-80% of the anterior myometrium. Case A woman with two previous caesarean sections presented a few weeks after her medical termination of pregnancy with vaginal bleeding. The diagnosis of retained products of conception from scar pregnancy was made after both ultrasound and MRI reported 'mass in the scar area'. Hysteroscopy showed no evidence of mass or retained products. While reviewing the MRI images, it was noted that the reported mass was not inside the endometrial cavity, but within the uterine wall near the scar area with loss of myometrial integrity extending up to the endometrial cavity, leaving a very thin layer of myometrium between the niche and endometrial lining. The final diagnosis of a caesarean scar niche was made and the patient was reassured because the possibility of scar pregnancy was ruled out. She was offered laparotomy to repair the niche if she wished to conceive again. Conclusion In our case, it is evident that the presence of mass misled the clinicians until the images were reviewed and the final diagnosis of caesarean scar niche was confirmed.
DO  - 10.1111/1471-0528.16710
