AB  - Introduction: Cesarean Scar Isthmocele (CSI) is associated with gynecological symptoms such as post-menstrual spotting, heavy menstrual bleeding, dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia and chronic pelvic pain and subfertility. It has been reported that approximately 30% of women with CSI experience postmenstrual spotting and abnormal uterine bleeding. Objective: To report a case of CSI with microdehiscence which was successfully repaired through transvaginal route. Method: Case report from a tertiary Hospital in Qatar. Patient/Results: This was a 28 years old, P1, who had previous 1 cesarean section 3 years back for breech presentation. She presented with very bothersome regular post menstrual brownish spotting and dysmenorrhea for more than 7 months. Saline hysterosonography showed CSI of 1.4x0.8 cms with overlying myometrium of 2.2 mm thickness. Saline was seen accumulating in vesico-uterine space through microdefects. She was also noted to have unicornuate uterus with no other associated anomalies. After discussion she underwent excision of the scar tissue and repair of the CSI through transvaginal route. Under general anesthesia, bladder was dissected off the cervix and uterus. Isthmocele was identified by transillumination with hysteroscope. The cesarean scar was excised and the defect was closed in two layers. She was reviewed after 6 months when her postmenstrual spotting and dysmenorrhea had significantly improved with improved ultrasound appearance with minimal defect and myometrial thickness of 5 mm. Conclusions: The significance of finding of microdehiscence of cesarean scar and the future risk of uterine rupture during pregnancy is not clear, due to lack of literature. However, if they are symptomatic, they will need treatment. Symptomatic cesarean scar isthmoceles with myometrial thickness of less than 3 mm are best treated with surgical resection and resuturing through laparoscopic or vaginal route. Repair through transvaginal route offers safe and minimally invasive technique to improve symptoms and potentially reduce the future risk of uterine rupture during pregnancy.
DO  - 10.1111/jog.14457
ER  -

