AB  - During pregnancy, the uterine mucosa (decidua) is highly populated by distinct NK cells, known as uterine or decidual NK (dNK) cells. Epidemiological and functional studies have linked dNK cells to pregnancy outcome because certain combinations of KIR and HLA genetic variants associate with birth weight and pregnancy complications. However, the decidual NK (dNK) cell niche remains poorly characterized. To dissect the human NK cell niche in pregnancy we have built a 43-marker mass cytometry panel. This panel combines phenotypic and functional markers to stain decidual samples from elective terminations of pregnancies and matched peripheral blood (pbNK). We show that combinations of tissue residency markers and KIR identify dNK subsets with distinct functional capacities, suggesting a compartmentalization of effector functions in the tissue. We go on to compare these findings with another tissue, the ascites of patients with high-grade serous ovarian cancer. Interestingly, the ascites contains KIR+ NK subsets that resemble both those from the decidua and peripheral blood. This study provides some insights into the unique and heterogeneous biology of tissue NK cells in pregnancy and cancer.
DO  - 10.1111/tan.13600
