AB  - Introduction: As the rate of caesarean delivery worldwide increases, the incidence of caesarean scar defect or isthmocele and the associated symptoms are also likely to be on rise. Isthmocele seem to be under diagnosed and surgical treatment protocols are currently not available. Objective: Therefore we wanted to evaluate the feasibility and the results of three surgical approaches in isthmocele repair: destruction by hysteroscopic by desiccation, resection and repair by laparoscopic or vaginal approach. Methods: Design: Prospective cohort study (Canadian Task Force II-2). Patients: 8 patients with previous caesarean were included between November 2014 and November 2016. All were highly symptomatic: intermenstrual bleeding and chronic pelvic pain. The thickness of the remaining muscular layer covering the defect (the safety wall) was diagnosed by transvaginal ultrasound and sonohysterography in all patients. In case of a safety wall >2.5 mm, HSC can be performed; otherwise, laparoscopy in case of an isthmocele located in the higher part of the cervix, vaginal approach if located in the lower part. 5 patients underwent hysteroscopic treatment; one had laparoscopy and 2 vaginal approach. Discussion and conclusions: Operative time was mostly similar to the time reported by others authors. The follow-up period ranged from 12 to 24 months. All patients experienced an improvement in menstruation duration, intermenstrual bleeding and pelvic pain except for one suffering from adenomyosis. One patient got pregnant. Three approaches have been proceeded successfully and safety. Hysteroscopy was minimally invasive and yielded good therapeutic results, provided that the safety wall was >2.5 mm. Conclusion: Surgical treatment must be considered in patients with symptomatic isthmocele. The choice of approach should be based according to the thickness of the residual myometrium, the localisation of the defect and the surgeon experience.
DO  - 10.1016/j.ejogrb.2018.08.440
