AB  - As the number of cesarean sections increases, gynecologists are increasingly confronted with the problem of caesarean scar defects (niches) . In the majority of cases, these niches are asymptomatic and do not require further therapy, especially in the absence of a continuing desire to have children . However, some women with niches have problems . These include, for example, postmenstrual spotting but also dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia and chronic lower abdominal pain . So far, the pathomechanisms have not been conclusively clarified . There are probably different pathomechanisms, and likely, different co-factors play a role . In cases of symptomatic niches or large scar dehiscences, various therapy options are available . The indication for surgery must be established individually . In addition to purely hysteroscopic methods, laparoscopic, vaginal or combined procedures are also available .
