AB  - Objective: To show a laparoscopic technique to repair the isthmocele when the myometrial thickness is below 2 mm or symptomatic isthmoceles previously corrected by hysteroscopy. Design: A case report. Settings: Academic hospital. Patients: A 32-year-old patient undergoing laparoscopy to to repair the isthmocele. Interventions: We present a 32 year-old woman without any medical precedents except for three cesarean sections and a tubarian sterilization. Her gynecological examinations were normal in the recent years, apart from persistent spotting and dysmenorrhea since the last c-section. Measurements/Results: Ultrasound exam showed a grade 2 isthmocele. For this reason an operative hysteroscopy was performed to correct the defect with the 5 mm hysteroscope and 5Fr bipolar electrode. During six months she remained asymptomatic, despite the image was still visible in the US. Then the postmentrual spotting began again. Conclusions: Laparoscopic correction of the isthmocele seems to be the gold standard for patients with reproductive desire or symptomatic isthmoceles previously corrected by hysteroscopy with a myometrial thickness below 2 mm. While laparoscopy is performed, the “Halloween sign” shows the extension and location of the scar defect. One month later, the patient continues without any symptoms or significant ultrasound findings. However, before further studies evaluate the efficacy of this method, the routine repair of cesarean scar defects cannot be recommended.
DO  - 10.1016/j.jmig.2018.09.701
