AB  - Objective: Caesarean scar defect, or isthmocele, is a reservoir type pouch on the anterior wall of the uterine isthmus caused by inappropriate healing of the lower segment incision after caesarean section. The incidence of caesarean scar defects is reported to be as high as 84%, and is increasing due to the rising rates of caesarean sections worldwide. Caesarean scar defects may be asymptomatic, or cause a number of gynaecologic and obstetric sequealae, including abnormal bleeding, pelvic pain, infertility and ectopic scar pregnancy. There is no current consensus on the ideal treatment for caesarean scar defects, with most evidence being limited to case reports. Design: A case report. Settings: An IVF center. Patients: This video demonstrates the case of a 32 year old female on a background of three previous elective caesarean sections who presented with a caesarean scar defect 3 months after a caesarean scar ectopic. Emergency laparoscopic surgery was performed by her initial treating team to evacuate her ectopic pregnancy. Follow up imaging with hysterosalpingogram confirmed the presence of a caesarean scar defect and was referred to Sydney Women's Endosurgery Centre (SWEC) for definitive treatment. Interventions: The patient underwent laparoscopic excision of the caesarean scar defect under hysteroscopic guidance. Measurements/Results: Results to be presented. Conclusions: A number of surgical modalities for treatment of CSD have been described in the literature, including hysteroscopic resection, vaginal revision and endometrial ablation. Treatment options largely depend on underlying symptoms and desire for future fertility. Laparoscopic excision remains the best chance of definitive therapy while maximising fertility potential.
DO  - 10.1016/j.jmig.2018.09.416
