AB  - OBJECTIVE: Evidence of microbiota within the intrauterine environment in normal pregnancies has spurred study of its origin and impact on the offspring. Collectively, such evidence challenges the presumption that a neonate is born sterile and alternately suggests that commensal microbiota colonize the fetus during gestation. To evaluate this hypothesis, we robustly characterized the fetal microbiota in a NHP model and determined its contribution to the gut microbiome in postnatal life. STUDY DESIGN: Offspring of Japanese macaque dams on a high-fat or control diet during pregnancy were sterilely delivered by Cesarean at gestational day G130 and necropsied. Swabs of the fetal colon were obtained (n=12), along with maternal anal and other body site swabs. Specimens of diet matched, vaginally delivered sibling macaques were obtained at 6, 10 months and 3 years (n=6, 14, 14). 16S rRNA gene sequencing and whole genome metagenomic sequencing was performed with negative kit contaminant and sequencing controls. Data was processed and analyzed using custom computational pipelines. RESULTS: Identified taxa first seen in the fetal colon were persistently the predominant constituents of the postnatal gut microbiome, up until at least 3 years of age (Fig 1A). A majority of the taxa at 3 years of age could be traced to low abundant species identified at G130, with the rest appearing primarily at 6 months. Inference of the metabolic pathways associated with the gut microbiota at each age (Fig 1B) demonstrates a unique functional profile at G130 compared later in life (Fig 1C). These differences included decreased capacity for replication and immune system interactions, and increased amino acid and xenobiotic metabolism (Fig 1D). CONCLUSION: These novel and likely impactful findings are the first direct evidence that expansion of taxa first present in fetal life contributes to the taxonomic diversity and functional capacity of the gut microbiome postnatally to 3 years of age. This highlights the importance of the gestational period to the development of the offspring microbiome and directly demonstrates that the same rare microbes first seen in fetal life rapidly expand their niche postnatally to populate the gut. This further implicates factors like maternal diet in shaping the health of the offspring long-term, as we have previously demonstrated. (Figure Presented).
