AB  - Use of thymidine analogs for the identification of proliferating cells is important in many fields of biology including developmental and regenerative biology, and cancer research as well as DNA replication mechanisms. Although fluorescence based detection is a standard practice which allows for multiplexing with other antibody based markers, there are occasions when colorimetric labeling is needed to avoid tissue auto-fluorescence, or to be compatible with standard histological stains for morphological and contextual information. In some labs only white light microscopes are available and conversion of fluorescence detection to a colorimetric signal is required. While BrdU (bromodeoxyuridine) proliferation detection is available as a colorimetric reagent, EdU (ethynyl-deoxyuridine), which is detected chemically using click chemistry, had not previously been reported to be detectable with colorimetric reagents. We report here the conversion of click chemistry based EdU detection from fluorescence to a colorimetric signal detectable with white light microscope, and the demonstration of labeling for multiplex detection of proliferation in dual pulse labeled EdU/BrdU tissue using horseradish peroxidase and alkaline phosphatase substrates. Dual pulse labeling with EdU and BrdU in the rat model treated with estradiol shows a wave of proliferation in both uterine and mammary tissue. Additional histological staining creates contextual environment to view the proliferating cells and localization within the tissue. Additionally, detection of EdU proliferating cells is shown to be compatible with stains such as Movat's pentachrome where the proliferating cells can readily be distinguished from the pentachrome stained nuclei, allowing for contextual identification of the niche environment where proliferation is occurring. We also demonstrate colorimetric EdU/BrdU double labeling in highly auto-fluorescent cardiac tissue in the mouse model when examining proliferation in heart valve development.
