AB  - OBJECTIVE: To investigate whether autologous cell therapy using CD133+ bone marrow derived stem cells (BMDSc) improve endometrial thickness and clinical results in patients with refractory Asherman's syndrome (AS) or endometrial atrophy (EA). DESIGN: Patients with refractory AS and EA were assessed by hysteroscopy, and BMDSc mobilization was performed by granulocyte-CSF (GCSF). Peripheral blood apheresis with isolation of CD133+ cells were obtained and delivered into the spiral arterioles of the patient by radiology intervention through each uterine artery using a micro-catheter. Subsequent hysteroscopy and assisted reproductive technologies (ART) were performed. MATERIALS AND METHODS: We recruited 16 patients aged 30-45 years who presented refractory AS and EA. A mean of 124.39 million cells (range 42-236) were instilled into the spiral arterioles. We recorded medical history, physical examination, vaginal ultrasound, histeroscopy, and histology before, 2, 3 and 6 months after cell therapy, then patients were observed for two months before attempted to conceive through ART. RESULTS: No major complications were reported. All 11 AS patients resumed menstruation after stem cell therapy and improved the uterine cavity after two months. Endometrial thickness increased from an average of 4,3 (range 2,7-5mm) to 6,7mm (range 3,1-12mm) after cell therapy. Likewise, four of the EA patients experienced a restored endometrial cavity and endometrial thickness increase from 4$2 (range 2$7-5mm) to 5$7mm (range 5- 12mm). The effects of treatment on endometrial growth and the recovery of menstruation appeared to be time sensitive, lasting around six months. Conception attempts following reconstruction of the endometrium resulted in two spontaneous pregnancy 2 and 4 months after cell therapy and seven positive pregnancies after 13 embryo transfers (ET) resulting in three biochemical pregnancies, two miscarriages at 9 and 17 weeks, one ectopic pregnancy, and two ongoing pregnancies. CONCLUSIONS: Autologous CD133+ BMDSCs transplanted into the endometrial stem cell niche seem to improve the endometrial cavity in patients with refractory AS and EA, for a six-month period. While pregnancies were achieved, functionality of the reconstructed endometrium remains to be improved. Nonetheless, CD133+ BMDSC autologous cell therapy is a promising option for patients with refractory AS and EA wishing to conceive.
