AB  - INTRODUCTION: Bacterial microbiome is considered an important player in the human body, accounting for 1-3% of the total body weight and resulting in 10 times more bacteria than human cells. Vaginal microbiome has been investigated but there is no data regarding the existence of endometrial microbiome. The aim of this work is to investigate the existence of human endometrial microbiome and its impact on endometrial receptivity and pregnancy in IVF patients. METHODS: In 19 IVF patients, two endometrial fluid (EF) samples were aspirated under sterile conditions. First EF before the endometrial biopsy for diagnosis of endometrial receptivity, and in the next cycle the second EF was obtained 24h before the embryo transfer. The endometrial microbiome was assessed by pyrosequencing of a pool of PCR product libraries that enclose the variable regions V3 to V5 of bacterial 16S rRNA gene, followed by taxonomical assignment. Endometrial receptivity array (ERA) was performed for the molecular diagnosis of the endometrial receptivity of the patient. RESULTS: Analysis of EF established differential microbiome profiles based on the bacterial species detected that can be classify into: (i) normal, >90% of Lactobacillus sp.; (ii) pathological, >10% of pathogens (eg. Atopobium, Gardnerella, sp.); and (iii) dysbiotic, >10% of bacteria changing the environmental endometrial niche (eg. Bifidobacterium sp.). Interestingly, patients with pathological microbiome even with receptive endometrium, had lower implantation rates or suffered early miscarriage compared to patients with normal microbiome. Furthermore, reversion of the pathological profile to a normal microbiome, resulted in improved pregnancy rates in receptive patients. CONCLUSIONS: The results from this pilot study provide novel information regarding the existence and functional relevance of different bacterial species in the human endometrium during the window of implantation. This opens new insights on the improvement of the endometrial factor in infertile patients. (Table Presented).
