AB  - Study Objective: To describe office hysteroscopy findings in a patient with cesarean section defect. Design: Case report. Setting: High Specialty South Central Hospital, PEMEX, Mexico city, Mexico. Patients: A 49 year old Woman, with 1 cesarean section, 1 abortion, 1 vaginal delivery (stillbirth), Diabetes Mellitus. Comes to check up, with abnormal bleeding 18 months after last normal menstrual cycle. Presenting variuos episodes of spotting with-out any other symptoms. During physical exploration, vaginal mucosa with no signs of lesions or atrophy. Cervix normal. It is performed an office hysteroscopy as part of routinely study protocol in patients with abnormal bleeding. Intervention: Office Hysteroscopy with a 3 mm, Versascope rigid lens. Measurements and Main Results: During the procedure, we have the next findings; redundant vaginal mucosa, external cervical os with little resistance, a normal endocervical canal. Internal cervical os with a dificcult entry for the lens due to estenotic tissue. A ''bubble'' shaped canal wich corresponds to isthmocele, and then a normal uterine cavity, with a thin endometrium, and normal ostiums. Conclusion: Office hysteroscopy as a part of study protocol is a reliable diagnostic option for patients with posmenopausal abnormal uterine bleeding.
