AB  - OBJECTIVE: Sampson's theory of retrograde menstruation remains the leading hypothesis for the pathogenesis of endometriosis. While most reproductive age women experience retrograde menstruation, the prevalence of endometriosis is only 7-10%. We hypothesize that endometriosis results from defects in the stem cell niche, causing more shedding of endometrial progenitors. Recently our group identified CD44 as one marker for isolation of endometrial epithelial progenitors (Stem Cells 2012, 4:808-22). The purpose of this study was to assess if uterine epithelial progenitor cells marked by CD44 are present at increased frequencies in ectopic endometrial implants. DESIGN: Case-control study. MATERIALS AND METHODS: The pathology specimens from 30 human endometriosis cases and 19 age matched EMB controls were obtained. EMB and endometriosis specimens were subdivided into proliferative phase or secretory phase based on pathologist review. All samples were immunostained for CD10, pan-keratin, and CD44. RESULTS: CD10 marked endometrial stroma while pan-keratin marked endometrial epithelia in all specimens. A population of CD44 positive endometrial epithelial cells was identified in 27/30 ectopic endometriosis implants. Further, there appeared to be an expansion of CD44 epithelial progenitors in the secretory phase of ectopic implants compared to the proliferative phase. This distribution of CD44 positive cells in ectopic endometriosis implants was similar to that seen in stage-matched normal endometrial tissue. CONCLUSION: CD44 positive epithelial progenitors are found in ectopic endometrial implants. Their distribution mirrored that seen in normal eutopic endometrium. Future work will focus on examining defects in the stem cell niche that may induce greater shedding of endometrial progenitors in patients with endometriosis.
DO  - 10.1016/j.fertnstert.2013.07.770
