AB  - OBJECTIVE: To report preliminary results of hysteroscopic treatment in patients with cesarean scar defect in order to correct the isthmocele and restore fertility. DESIGN: Prospective, observational and analytic study. MATERIALS AND METHODS: Prospective study performed from August 2008 through October 2012. 11 patients were included, who had at least one previous cesarean delivery. In all cases, diagnosis of isthmocele was performed with transvaginal ultrasound. Diagnosis was confirmed by hysteroscopy. Surgical treatment consisted of resection of the inferior and superior edges of the defect. The scar tissue was removed. RESULTS: Mean patient age was 36,72 (28-44). Isthmocele was identified in all patients. Hysteroscopic treatment was performed without complications. Histologic analysis of tissue samples informed fibrotic scar and inflammatory tissue. No complications such as ectopic pregnancy, Ashermann syndrome or vesicouterine fistula were recorded. After surgery, 1 patient became pregnant spontaneously and 8 patients underwent embryo transfer resulting in 4 evolutive pregnancies. CONCLUSION: In spite of not knowing completely the mechanism of isthmocele formation, it has a detrimental impact in fertility. Our preliminary results show that surgical treatment of the defect is associated with an improvement in reproductive outcomes.
DO  - 10.1016/j.fertnstert.2013.07.681
