AB  - Uterine Isthmocele is a sacular cavity located at the anterior wall of the uterine isthmus. It is secondary to cesarean-section scar defect, and can be a cause of postmenstrual abnormal uterine bleeding and secondary infertility, due to the presence of blood stained cervical mucus that affect sperm quality and fluid-reflux into the uterine cavity that impairs endometrial implantation. Clinical manifestations will depend on the size of the isthmocele and the position of the uterus. The diagnosis can be made with Transvaginal Ultrasound and Sonohysterography. Surgical strategy consists in the resection of the lower margin of the isthmocele, which lead to an increase in the diameter of the cervical canal, thus facilitating blood drainage during the menstrual period. In this video we show how to perform the resectoscopic treatment of uterine isthmocele, a minimally invasive surgery that can solve abnormal uterine bleeding and increase spontaneous conception rate.
