AB  - Stem cells Stem cells are primitive and rare undifferentiated cells that have the ability to self-renew as well as produce more differentiated daughter cells. Their rarity and lack of distinguishable morphological features and specific markers make it difficult to identify their locations in tissues. Stem cells can be broadly divided into two categories: embryonic and adult. Embryonic stem cells are found in the inner cell mass of the blastocyst, whereas adult stem cells are found in an undifferentiated state throughout the whole body after embryonic development. Embryonic and adult stem cells are classified by their ability to differentiate into cells of different cell lineage. While stem cells derived from the zygote are totipotent, and therefore able to generate any kind of embryonic germ layer, as well as extra-embryonic tissue, adult stem cells are multipotent that is capable of producing only cells of the same germ lineage or unipotent, which means able to form only one cellular type of the same lineage. Adult stem cells are found in anatomic structures called the niches. The stem cell niche is a microenvironment of surrounding support stem cell that signal to the stem cell population. The niche cells provide signals that maintain stem cells in an undifferentiated state, protecting them from differentiation, proliferation, and apoptotic cues. However, they also sense the need for tissue replacement and communicate proliferative and differentiation signals to resident stem cells. Maintenance of stem cell population requires cellular self-renewal that is the capacity to generate identical daughter cells. Alternatively, stem cells can undergo asymmetric cell division, producing an identical daughter cell and a more differentiated daughter or symmetric division producing two daughter stem cells or two transit amplifying (TA) progenitors. The TA cells undergo rapid proliferation and expansion of cell numbers, ultimately leading to terminal differentiation into various functional cells with no further proliferative potentials. Several assays have been used and tested to identify stem cells. In vitro assays include demonstration of the cell's clonogenicity, differentiation and proliferation potential, as well as phenotypic features. In vivo assays include tissue reconstitution as the ideal test, and the demonstration of label retaining cells (LRCs). Therefore, in order to accurately characterize a population of stem cells, it is necessary to apply several assays with consistent methodologies, demonstrating the cell's renewal capacity and its ability to differentiate into mature cells. Endometrial Stem/Progenitor Cells (SPCs) The human uterus mainly consists of the endometrium and the outer smooth muscle layer termed myometrium. The endometrium is broadly composed of two cell types; the glandular epithelial cells and the supporting mesenchymal cells, which include stromal fibroblasts, the vasculature, and leukocytes. Functionally, the endometrium is composed of two layers; the outer functionalis layer and the inner basalis layer. The functionalis comprising the upper two thirds is composed of dense glandular tissue surrounded by a loose connective stroma. The inner basalis layer rests on the muscular subendometrial myometrium and contains primarily the base of the glands, dense stroma, and large vessels. The basalis layer serves as a germinal compartment for generating the new functionalis each cycle. Recently, it has been postulated that the process of cellular regeneration may depend on stem cells located in specific niches of endometrium. According to this model, these cells migrate to other places and generate groups of progenitor cells with the potential to differentiate into epithelial, stromal, and vascular cells when submitted to certain stimuli, such as high levels of estradiol. Chan et al presented the first evidence for the existence of endometrial stem/ progenitor cells (SPCs) by identifying clonogenic human endometrial epithelial and stromal cells. They showed that the human endometrium contains a small group of cells with clonogenic capacity, comprised of a small percentage of epithelial cells (0.22%) and stromal cells (1.25%). However, it has been also showed that frequency of clonogenic epithelial and stromal cells did not vary in different phases of the menstrual cycle or in inactive endometrium. Since inactive endometrium does not contain functional endometrium, authors postulated that the putative endometrial SPCs reside in the basalis layer and persist beyond the menopause. Other studies have demonstrated that endometrial SPCs, when cultured in medium specifically formulated to promote cellular differentiation, are multipotent and able to differentiate into endometrial glandular and stromal cells, adipocytes, osteoblasts, leiomyocytes, chondrocytes, cardiomyocytes, and dopamine producing neurons. There are no specific known markers for endometrial SPCs that distinguish them from their mature progeny. Recent studies showed that Oct-4, Musashi-1, c-Kit, SALL4, Notch-1 and ABCG2 might be the markers of endometrial SPCs. However, further analyses require whether these markers are specific for endometrial SPCs. Recent studies revealed that in addition to fetal stem cells, bone marrow may be another source of endometrial SPCs. This hypothesis is appealing as bone marrow-derived cells (BMDCs) have been shown to circulate and be able to differentiate into multiple cell types, including endothelial cells, hepatocytes, neurons, skin, cardiomyocytes and gastrointestinal epithelium. Taylor suggested that BMDCs could be involved in the process of endometrium regeneration in transplanted patients. These findings were further corroborated by studies using a murine model of bone marrow transplantation, demonstrating that endometrial cells can originate from male donor-derived bone marrow cells and suggest that nonuterine stem cells contribute to the generation of endometrial tissue. Recently, Ikoma et al reported that BMDCs from human male donors can differentiate into endometrial glands in female transplant recipients. Stem cells and Endometriosis Results of the recent studies suggest that endometrial SPCs may play a role in the pathogenesis of endometriosis. Sasson and Taylor proposed that endometrial SPCs residing in the basalis layer can be shed through the fallopian tube to the peritoneal cavity during menses, and establish endometriotic implants. They also suggested that SPCs derived from either the bone marrow, endometrium, or an alternate source may be responsible for the observations that support the theory of coelomic metaplasia. To date, no direct evidence for the role of endometrial SPCs in the pathogenesis of human endometriosis has been reported. However, there is indirect evidence for the role of stem cells in the pathogenesis of endometriosis. Studies have already showed that unfractioned human endometrial cells establish ectopic endometrial growth in many models used for endometriosis. It has been reported that significantly more basalis layer was shed in the menstrual flow, suggesting an increased number of SPCs in this layer that can result in a propensity for endometriosis. The ability of BMDCs to regenerate both eutopic and ectopic endometrium was also demonstrated in an experimental murine model. In this study, female mice received ectopic endometrial grafts and male mouse-derived BMDCs, which could be traced by fluorescent in situ hybridization directed to the Y chromosome. Donor BMDCs were detected in both eutopic and ectopic endometrial tissues suggestint that BMDCs may not only contribute to maintaining uterine endometrial regeneration, but also generating endometriosis. Conclusion Endometrial SPCs may play important roles in both the physiologic and pathologic processes. Physiologically they are likely involved in the regeneration of shed endometrium in each menstrual cycle, and they may also be involved in the pathogenesis of endometriosis. However, involvement of SPCs in the pathogenesis of endometriosis is a new hypothesis and requires further studies.
