AB  - The feasibility of hepatocyte replacement treatment for liver failure hinges on the availability of a safe and abundant supply. We hypothesise that by recreating the microenvironmental niche of the developing liver, amniotic epithelial cells (hAEC) can be coaxed to become functional hepatocytelike cells. hAECs were harvested from placentas of term pregnancy Cesarean section deliveries. Cells were cultured by primary explant technique onto plastic with DMEM. Hepatocyte differentiation was performed with three-day induction step using Activin-A and FGF-4 followed by 10 days differentiation using factors including HGF, Oncostatin-M, DMSO, FGF-1 and FGF-4. hAECs have mixed mesenchymal-epithelial features at dissociation. Upon differentiation, hAEC trans-differentiated into hepatocytelike cells with polygonal morphology. RT-PCR analysis shows upregulation for Albumin, EpCAM, E-cadherin, CYP1A2, CYP2E1 and CYP3A4 compared to undifferentiated cells. This was confirmed with immunofluorescence showing positivity and co-localisation of EpCAM, Albumin, E-Cadherin and HNF-4. Functional testing shows positive glycogen storage within cytoplasm with PAS stain, and free albumin secretion into the supernatant using ELISA assay. Co-culture of these cells with fetal liver non-parenchymal cells to recreate the liver niche greatly enhanced the hepatocytic function of these cells. Intra-peritoneum transplant into C57BL6 mice with Jo-2 induced liver injury showed engraftment of human-specific albumin positive cells within parenchyma adjacent to portal tracts. In conclusion, hAECs were able to differentiate into functional hepatocyte-like cells when cultured in-vitro with factors in the natural environment of a developing liver. These cells are potentially immunologically naiïve, abundant in supply and offer an exciting source for generation of functionally useful hepatocytes.
